\par\par \b \uc2 Chance of Breakout in Gold, Says Phillip Futures -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2014\par 05:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0341 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Gold appears to be facing resistance at $1,323/oz, while support sits at $1,312/oz in the short-term, writes Howie Lee, an investment analysts with Phillip Futures, in a report. Both the upper and lower bands were tested overnight, he says. Based on technical indicators, there is a "chance that gold may exhibit a huge breakout in the near future," Lee says. Dovish comments last week from U.S. Federal Reserve Chair Janet Yellen about U.S. economic growth, downbeat economic data from the U.S. overnight and geopolitical tensions in Iraq, could help provide a floor to precious metal prices. Spot gold is at $1,316.20/oz, down $2.20 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @ArpanMukherjee)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0339 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Political risk consultancy Trusted Sources is skeptical that a strong rally in Thailand equities is justified after the May 22 coup, while narrowing polls in Indonesia's presidential race is reason to go neutral on the country, says chief economist Larry Brainard. "If Prabowo prevails and wins we would see a lot of people taking money off the table, particularly in the fixed income area. If Jokowi wins people will still be cautious but you'll see certain sectors doing well" such as infrastructure, Mr. Brainard says, referring to Indonesia's two presidential candidates. However, he is not fazed by recent falls in the rupiah triggered by oil price rises following a conflict in Iraq. "It's basically a short-term trade and I think we should just look through that," said Mr. Brainard. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_)\par \par \uc2 0323 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Thailand's benchmark stock index will likely trade sideways in the short term but as Thailand's economic fundamentals begin to catch up with expectations about a recovery in 2014 and 2015, the longer term outlook is more positive, KGI Securities says. "SET has no near-term conviction and may continue to consolidate, amid slowing foreign equity inflows and [the] absence of fresh local macro news," the brokerage says. It recommends investors to accumulate stocks on any dips, forecasting a 1500-1550 range for the third quarter of 2014. The SET trades 0.3% higher early Thursday at 1473, or 4.1% higher in the month-to-date. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_)\par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Narrowing polls in Indonesia support the case for shorting the rupiah, Nomura says in a note. "The latest surveys highlight an increasing uncertainty regarding the presidential election outcome," it says. "In our view, this most recent increase in political uncertainty also raises the risk of capital outflows, especially given the considerable accumulation of foreign bond and equity positioning since end-2013." Nomura says \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step up its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the rupiah falling much further, but with weak reserves, BI will likely adopt "a relatively light-handed approach." The rupiah has depreciated 3.7% against the U.S. dollar in the month-to-date, far more than most other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which are mostly stronger or flat. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] As the rupiah weakens, investors can be expected to show interest in companies earning U.S. dollars, says \uc2 Trimegah Securities\uc2 . It is most bullish on oil and gas services stocks, such as \uc2 PT Wintermar Offshore Marine\uc2  (WINS.JK), \uc2 PT Logindo Samudramakmur\uc2  (LEAD.JK) and \uc2 PT Elnusa\uc2  (ELSA.JK). It recommends investors exercise caution with companies with rupiah earnings but U.S. dollar or Euro debt, such as PT Japfa Comfeed (JPFA.JK). The rupiah trades 0.2% stronger against the U.S. dollar Thursday, but has lost 3.5% in the month to date. This comes as oil price increases due to conflict in Iraq turn investors away out of concern that import costs will rise and Indonesia's current-account deficit will become more of a drag on growth. (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Australian job vacancies rose for the second quarter in a row in the three months to the end of May, which should lend some encouragement to the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  which, recently, has been downbeat on the outlook for job creation. Job vacancies rose 2.5% over the quarter, building on a 2.8% rise in the prior three months, according to government statistics. RBA Assistant Governor \uc2 Chris Kent\uc2  said last week he expects unemployment to remain elevated for the next two years. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Nikkei is up 0.3% at 15314.74 midday. Volume is relatively light, under 1.0 billion shares. "The speech by Prime Minister Abe (made Tuesday evening) turned out to be a total 'non-event', and so post-event activity (Wednesday and Thursday) is also very subdued," says an equity trading director at a European brokerage. "Abe's economic revitalization plan lacked a lot of detail, so investors are keen to see, for example, the exact amount by which corporate tax rates will be lowered, how the revenue will be made up elsewhere, and by precisely how much the government pension body (GPIF) will increase its exposure to stocks. Valuation-wise, Japan shares still lag their overseas counterparts, so this kind of incremental buy pressure could continue for weeks or more." (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China Gas Holdings' (0384.HK) full-year earnings released late Wednesday contained few surprises but its bullish long-term guidance, such as a forecast for a 24% increase in natural gas sales this year, has investors abuzz. "Growth prospects remain bright," Jefferies says, noting two pipelines are set to be \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, boosting volumes. Analysts back their buy rating on the stock and raise their price target by about a quarter, to HK$15.90. Shares rise 3.5% to HK$14.36. (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0250 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Southeast Asian stocks trade mostly higher early Thursday, led by the Philippines PSEi, which heads for a fresh 12-month high and is up 0.4% in line with improved sentiment in the wider region. Gainers include \uc2 Philex Mining Corp\uc2 . (PX.PH) and Jolibee Foods Corp. (JFC.PH), up 2.3% and 1.8% respectively. Elsewhere, Indonesia's JSX and Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index gain 0.3% each. Malaysia's KLCI is down 0.2%. On the data front, Singapore reports industrial production data for May later in the day. "We are forecasting a slower growth rate of 3.9% year-on-year in Singapore's May IP, compared to the average of 8.4% year-on-year in the first four months of this year," UOB says. "The unexpected contraction in Singapore's May non-oil domestic exports is likely to show up in the manufacturing numbers today." (jake.watts@wsj.com; @jmwatts_)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/CNY falls slightly to track a lower central parity set by the PBOC. The pair is at 6.2314 vs 6.2344 at Wednesday's close, after the central parity is set at 6.1538 vs 6.1555 Wednesday, tracking the dollar's broad weakness overseas. "There are still substantial amounts of dollar purchases from importers, but they failed to support the dollar's prices today," says a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.2390/6.2440 from 6.2412/6.2458 late Wednesday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Taiwan government bonds are higher in thin trade, mostly reflecting overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, says a local trader. Market participants are focussed on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly policy meeting later Thursday. Most people don't expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to change its monetary policy, but will seek cues from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's comments about the outlook for inflation. "We will watch whether the governor thinks the rising inflationary pressure is temporary or not," the trader adds. The five-year bond yield is at 1.1825% vs 1.2020% late Wednesday, the trader tips the yield to trade between 1.1800% and 1.2000% for the session. The 10-year bond yield is at 1.6000% vs 1.6200% Wednesday. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0239 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/IDR has barely come off Wednesday's 4-month peak of 12,090 despite the overnight slump of the U.S. dollar index on much weaker-than-expected U.S. 1Q Gross Domestic Product data. The persistent biddish tone in USD/IDR is likely to keep the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its toes, ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been suspected of intermittently selling U.S. dollars and buying the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rupiah's decline this week, said a couple of traders. The charts suggest that if USD/IDR remains above 11,980 by the end of the day, the daily Bollinger uptrend channel will remain in play - an indication that the dollar is still likely to rise versus the rupiah in the near term. USD/IDR is now 12,090 from its Wednesday close of 12,085. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow 