\par\par \b \uc2 Rupiah Drops:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Support, Prabowo Closes Gap -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shuli Ren  \par \uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2014\par 17:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah fell to the lowest level since February today, trading at 12,135 per dollar this morning. The \uc2 iShares MSCI Indonesia ETF\uc2  (EIDO) fell 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 The decline is partly explained by \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's decision to temporarily "undervalue" rupiah to boost exports. But the newest election polls are also at play. Presidential candidate Prabowo Subianto is narrowing his lead and may even be slightly ahead of the frontrunner Joko Widodo, known as "Jokowi", two weeks ahead of the presidential election on July 9.\par \par \uc2 The PolComm survey, released yesterday, shows that the margin of victory for Jokowi has shrunk to just 3.1%. About 10.3% of voters were still undecided. Jokowi earlier in the year had a 20% lead.\par \par \uc2 Oddly, and unique in Indonesia, some of the election polls have been withheld from publication, for fear that Jokowi voters may shift away to Mr. Prabowo. The Sydney Morning Herald reports:\par \par \uc2 A number of sources contacted by \uc2 Fairfax Media\uc2  have confirmed that three credible polling organisations have now measured the gap between the two candidates at either within the margin of error, or with Mr Prabowo in the lead.\par \par \uc2 A Prabowo win may trigger capital outflow and put pressure on rupiah, which \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will tolerate anyhow, according to Nomura Securities:\par \par \uc2 This most recent increase in political uncertainty also raises the risk of capital outflows, especially given the considerable accumulation of foreign bond and equity positioning since end-2013. We note that foreign investors have invested about USD3.8bn in local equities and an additional USD7.5bn in local government bonds since the beginning of 2014. Having said that, there is a risk that \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BI) could step up its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest further IDR depreciation. However, given the relatively weak \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves adequacy ratios (about six months of import coverage, or 2x short-term external debt), we think BI is likely to adopt a relatively light-handed approach to lean against IDR depreciation rather than attempting to stem it. This is also consistent with yesterday"s comments from BI officials that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is comfortable with the recent IDR weakness, as it supports exports competitiveness while curbing imports demand. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog,  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/ }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 25, 2014 11:40 ET (15:40 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140625ea6p002g5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
