\par\par \b \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  Bullish on Rupiah Ahead of July Polls -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,925 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2014\par 04:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0338 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects Indonesia's rupiah to outperform its regional peers, particularly within the period up to the July presidential election. It signifies a vote of confidence in the region's worst-performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2013 amid still-wobbly sentiment on emerging markets and reflects how banks now have to consider emerging-market economies on an individual basis rather than as a broad category. It cites recent improvement in Indonesia's trade balance and attractive yields of the government's rupiah bonds as the reasons for its constructive view and marks the first time the bank has turned bullish on the rupiah since before last summer. It forecasts the USD/IDR to fall to 11,800 in the second quarter vs 12,115 currently. (I-made.sentana@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0337 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY will likely try to rise above 104.00 on Thursday if the FOMC later in the day meets market expectations by further shrinking its bond-buying program, says a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo. "For equity players, such an decision would be no surprise so the market won't react to it too much," he says, adding "but some bond players had expected that the Fed may not taper this time due to confusion in emerging markets," which could mean higher rates on U.S. Treasurys if the Fed does indeed taper, which in turn should benefit the USD/JPY. The pair is at 103.21.(takashi.mochizuki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The NZD/USD has moved higher over the session "on a further lessening of emerging market contagion risk," says Imre Speizer, senior strategist at \uc2 Westpac Bank\uc2 . The pair is at 0.8279 versus 0.8262 early in New Zealand. Emerging market jitters receded after \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased interest rates in an attempt to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Markets are now looking ahead to the results of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's first policy-setting meeting of the year, due early Thursday New Zealand time. The Fed is expected to continue scaling back its monthly bond-buying program by another $10 billion to $65 billion--Speizer says anything over $10 billion will be "U.S. dollar positive and will weigh on the Kiwi." The FOMC announcement will be followed by the \uc2 New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate decision where there is a 30% chance of a rate hike, he says. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 0316 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY could fall to as low as 102.50 in Asia, as Japanese exporters are expected to continue selling the pair on this end-of-month trading day for the spot market, says a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo. He adds trading is likely to be quiet ahead of the FOMC's announcement later in the day. The pair is at 103.16.(takashi.mochizuki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY daily chart indicates technical support is growing for the dollar, reinforcing demand for the greenback as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meets to decide on monetary policy. The daily Ichimoku Cloud support--a technical charting tool that forecasts support and resistance levels--now props up the dollar at 102.88. Investors, who are bullish on the dollar but are waiting for a chance to buy it cheaper might place their orders near this technical support level. If the Cloud support holds up Wednesday, USD/JPY could rally off this base and toward the 20-day Bollinger mid resistance line at 104.00 in the days ahead. The Fed may decide later today to again reduce the amount of bonds it purchases; many analysts expect a gradual but consistent pullout from quantitative easing through the rest of this year. In December, the Fed announced a $10 billion reduction of its $85 billion a month bond purchase program. USD/JPY is now 103.16 versus Tuesday's close of 102.95. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China shares are higher as banks extend gains in bargain hunting following recent losses, say analysts. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.3% at 2044.71, with analysts tipping the index to hover around 2050. "The range-bound pattern may extend into the last two sessions before a week-long holiday," says Guo Feng, chief investment adviser with Northeast Securities. Shares of companies listed on the ChiNext board, a marketplace for startups, stabilize following the recent pullback, which may lend support to market sentiment, Guo adds. Among actives, banks have risen more as China avoided a potentially destabilizing hit to its financial system after a major shadow lender Monday arranged a bailout for buyers of an investment product that was on the verge of going bust; China Minsheng Banking (600016.SH) adds 1.5% to CNY7.30 while AgBank (601288.SH) gains 0.8% to CNY2.39. The Shenzhen Composite Index is up 0.1% at 1080.60 amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Turkish lira may have bounced back spectacularly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked the overnight lending rate by a jaw-dropping 4.5 percentage points to 12.0%, but some traders are turning skeptical that this is the answer to Turkey's problems. The issue at heart is Turkey's wide current account deficit, said one emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in Singapore. At nearly 8% of economic output, the country's debt is at levels that Greece was at before its economic collapse. For an Asia perspective, India, another emerging market that recently has been dogged by current account deficit woes, is expected to see its deficit reduced to 2.5% of GDP for the current financial year. The India rupee has managed to sustain its recovery from its record lows seen in August 2013 because the government implemented policies to clamp down on gold and oil imports in order to fix the current account deficit - which is what Turkey now needs to do. Unless more concrete steps are taken to bolster the stopgap measure of hiking interest rates to prevent outflows, the Turkish lira could surrender its gains and head back toward the record low of 2.3900 notched on Monday. USD/TRY is now 2.2485 versus its Monday close of 2.2800. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Philippine peso is up alongside other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover across the board following \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiking rates sharply to stem its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. USD/PHP may head back down toward the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 44.93 as risk appetite improves. Investor panic may have subsided for now, which could allow the peso to recover losses, but traders are still cautious as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meets later in the day to decide on whether to reduce quantitative easing again. If the Fed announces another reduction in bond buying, U.S. bond yields would rise and lift the greenback as well. USD/PHP is now 45.14 from Tuesday's close of 45.23. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The rupiah soared to 12,115 versus the U.S. dollar early Wednesday--its strongest since Jan. 21--as emerging market jitters ease. USD/IDR has now rejected the bullish technical signal that had appeared Monday as emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stage a global rebound on the back of the Turkish lira rocketing higher after \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced massive interest rate hikes to stem losses for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Investors had been worried that the Indonesia rupiah could be the next target for speculative investors looking to profit from the emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rout. Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week by \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped to prevent the rupiah from falling much and has also helped avert investor panic, says an onshore trader. But a U.S. dollar rebound in the near term cannot be ruled out as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meets later in the day to decide on whether to reduce quantitative easing again. If the Fed announces another reduction in bond buying, U.S. bond yields would rise and lift the greenback as well. USD/IDR is now 12,120 from Tuesday's close of 12,185. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Malaysia ringgit rallies 0.4% Wednesday in sync with other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia as investors respond to the recovery of the Turkish lira overnight. USD/MYR has now fallen out of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel--negating the previous bullish-dollar bias and may soon head for the trading barrier at 3.3000. \uc2 Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets today for its interest-rate policy decision. Most analysts expect no change on rates. The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  policy meeting later on Wednesday may be more influential on USD/MYR as the street expects the Fed to announce another reduction in bond buying. If the Fed buys fewer bonds the benchmark \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields will rise and lift the dollar. USD/MYR is now 3.32220 from its Tuesday close of 3.3340. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Gold is set to lose a major price support as China winds down for the Lunar New Year and its gold demand remains absent in ensuing weeks, Victor Thianpiriya, commodity strategist at ANZ says in a report. "We are cautious of chasing rallies in this environment," he says. Spot gold is under pressure and is now at $1,253/oz, down $2.70 from its previous close. After rising to a 10-week high Monday, the sharp pullback in prices "highlights how precarious rallies are in the short-term," he says. Also, premiums in China over the global prices which have declined to $7/oz on Monday from $20/oz in early January indicates "a loss of interest," he says. Besides, easing of import restrictions on gold by India is unlikely before the end of February, when the country's federal budget is presented in Parliament, he says. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 28 Jan 2014 22:38 EDT  \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2   Bullish on Rupiah Ahead of July -2-\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 is up 0.6% at 5207 as emerging markets jitters receded after \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked interest rates to stem exchange rate depreciation. Cyclical stocks are reversing Tuesday's under performance, while high-dividend yield and defensive stocks are lagging. "Recent concern about emerging markets is eerily similar to last May, when we had a falling Aussie dollar, worries about China's shadow banking system and expectations of Fed tapering," says Morgans investment adviser Christopher Macdonald. "The big difference this time is that the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  have upped their expectations for global growth. All the major economies are pulling in the right direction." He expects the weaker Aussie dollar to attract offshore support for Australian blue chips. BHP (BHP.AU) gains 1.2%, while CBA (CBA.AU) is up 0.4% and \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) falls 0.5%. Focus turns to President Obama's State of the Union address and the FOMC meeting later Wednesday. (david.rogers@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 28, 2014 22:38 ET (03:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140129ea1t000ft\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
