\par\par \b \uc2 USD/Asia Consolidates in the Eye of the Storm -- Asia Daily  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 2,048 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2014\par 02:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY    6.0406-6.0500   6.0348-6.0521 \par USD/TWD    30.210-30.500   30.000-30.800 \par USD/KRW     1,080-1,090     1,075-1,100 \par USD/SGD    1.2719-1.2766   1.2700-1.2800 \par USD/MYR    3.3300-3.3470   3.3230-3.3500 \par USD/THB     32.80-32.91     32.50-33.00 \par USD/PHP     45.21-45.45     45.00-45.50 \par USD/IDR    12,220-12,278   12,050-12,300 \par USD/INR     63.00-63.50     62.87-63.70 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY--consolidation. The PBOC may move the USD/CNY benchmark exchange rate slightly higher in reaction to the blip up in the U.S. dollar index overnight. The yuan could weaken slightly in the spot market if the PBOC benchmark rate is set above 6.1050; Monday's fixing was 6.1022. The yuan is expected to be untainted by the wave of risk aversion that has seen sharp losses in other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , due to the mindset that the Chinese authorities' vice-like grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will prevent a collapse, and also because holding the yuan is a long-term trade that is a really bet on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liberalization -- considered a eventuality. Trading activity in the yuan will soon taper off as the Lunar New Year holidays approach this week. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/CNY is at 6.0406 (record low), before 6.0348 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 6.0500 (psychological resistance), then at 6.0521 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.0600 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD--upside bias. USD/TWD may keep climbing as the dollar surpasses 30.500 briefly on Monday - which could spook those who are still short the dollar. The growing risk aversion in emerging market is less likely to affect the North Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as much, due to the stronger balance sheets of these countries and less reliance on foreign inflows related to quantitative easing. However, the technical upside bias for USD/TWD will persist as long as the dollar stays above 30.210 and thereby still within the Bollinger uptrend channel. Generally, the growing risk aversion in emerging markets worldwide is likely to keep the greenback well-bid in Asia. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 30.210 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 30.000 (psychological support), before 29.930 (20-day Bollinger mid support). Immediate resistance is likely at 30.500 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel and psychological resistance), before 30.800 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW--pullback and consolidation. USD/KRW is retreating from its 4-month high of 1,087.7 as the dollar appears slightly overbought on the daily chart. The greenback on Monday was trading beyond the confines of the upper Bollinger band (standard deviation 2) - a sign that the recent fluctuation has exceeded the typical level of volatility and thus will revert towards the median. USD/KRW could hence slip under the psychological barrier at 1,080 and then consolidate there. However, continued weakness in equities may spark outflows that could weigh on the won again. Asia stocks are starting the day with a weak tone on the heels of a slight drop in U.S. equities overnight. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,080 (psychological support), then at 1,075 (base of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1,074 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2). Immediate resistance is at 1,090 (psychological resistance), then at 1,100 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/SGD--consolidation lower. The technical upside bias for USD/SGD has been negated as the Singapore dollar received a boost Monday - likely due to safe haven inflows. The USD/SGD may slip toward the next technical support at 1.2719 as investors shirk high-yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region in favor of more stable ones like the Sing dollar. The Singapore dollar is recognized by most investors as a regional safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , due to the country's strong economic fundamentals, and thus tends to hold its value better in times of risk aversion. Traders are currently looking at relative-value trades within Asia that entail betting on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Singapore dollar to do well against regional peers. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2719 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 1.2700 (psychological support), before 1.2668 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1.2766 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2800 (psychological resistance), before 1.2821 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR--consolidation. USD/MYR is likely to subside from its 4-year low of 3.3465 touched on Monday as a reprieve in the emerging market selloff appears. The dollar is a tad weaker in Asia early Tuesday as equities attempt to recover from the recent slide, helped by bargain-hunting. But the upcoming U.S. Federal Reserve meeting, which could result in another reduction in bond-buying, will keep investors skittish about being short the dollar. As an indication of speculative intent in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the offshore-traded USD/MYR nondeliverable forward contract is signalling a taming of demand for dollars - which could mean risk aversion is subsiding. The price premium that the 1-month USD/MYR contract commands over the spot contract price has dropped slightly from around MYR 75 to MYR 65. Onshore, traders suspect the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday near 3.3470 to stem further ringgit weakness. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.3300 (psychological support), then at 3.3230 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 3.3200 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 3.3470 (suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level and top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel), before 3.3500 (psychological resistance and top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB--consolidation. Fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept the baht well-behaved even as the emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  selloff brews and the political protests in Bangkok escalate due to gunshot casualties at Thai polling stations. The recent stability of the baht despite increasing violence at the anti-government rallies in Bangkok is likely due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quell investor panic, said one trader. The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  is rumoured to be capping USD/THB at 33.10-33.15 and has very large offers placed near those levels to prevent the dollar from rising against the baht. This has been deterring speculative attacks on the baht, which has been trading in a narrow range of 32.75-33.00 for the past week. Technical upside bias for USD/THB could be reinstated if the dollar climbs back into the daily Bollinger uptrend channel that now caps at 32.91. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.80 (psychological support), before 32.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 32.91 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 33.00 (psychological resistance), before 33.13 (4-year high).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP--consolidation lower. The Philippine peso could recoup some losses as USD/PHP may ease from its 3.5-year high due to technical resistance capping near 45.40 and the relief rally seen in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  early Tuesday. Though the emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  panic is not yet over, there might be an 'eye of the storm' approaching as the daily charts suggest the dollar may be acquiring an overbought skew in the short term. USD/PHP has been trading slightly above the upper Bollinger band (standard deviation 2), a sign that the recent fluctuation has exceeded the typical level of volatility and thus will revert towards the median. But unless USD/PHP pulls back sharply this week, technical analysis of the monthly chart suggests USD/PHP could still rally much higher in the medium term if it ends January above 42.21 and thereby above the Ichimoku Cloud support. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 45.21 (monthly Ichimoku Cloud support), then at 45.00 (psychological support), before 44.81 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 45.45 (Monday's 3.5-year peak), before 45.50 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR--consolidation higher. USD/IDR entered the daily Bollinger uptrend channel Monday - a bullish technical signal - but it remains to be seen if the dollar can sustain its rally Tuesday. Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are starting to recoup some losses early Tuesday, possibly due to profit-taking of bearish positions or opportunistic buying of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on dips. If the bullish Bollinger uptrend channel is upheld by the end of the day, the dollar could rally much higher, possibly toward 12,390. Investors may be worried that the Indonesia rupiah could be the next target for speculative investors looking to profit from the emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rout. The fear of risk aversion spreading to the rupiah likely stems from sharp consecutive falls in the India rupee over the past two days. The rupee bears similar traits to the Indonesia rupiah because of its high-yielding nature. The rupee and rupiah were both favoured investments that enjoyed large inflows related to U.S. quantitative easing and may now pullback sharply as the U.S. Federal Reserve reduces its pace of bond buying. The main reason why the rupiah hasn't weakened much so far is \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avert investor panic; an onshore trader said \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling dollars in the market to prevent it from rallying too quickly against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Punters, however, will be placing their bets on the USD/IDR nondeliverable forward contracts, which are beyond the influence of \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  and in this way reflect better market sentiment for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 12,220 (base of\par \par \uc2 27 Jan 2014 20:22 EDT  USD/Asia Consolidates in the Eye of the Storm -- -2-\par \par \uc2 daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 12,050 (20-day Bollinger mid support), before 12,000 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 12,278 (5-year high), before 12,300 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/INR--pullback and consolidation. USD/INR could retreat lower due to a technical signal that the U.S. dollar has been overbought in the short term. Monday's spike above the upper Bollinger band (standard deviation 2) suggests positioning is overly skewed towards long-USD, which could prompt profit-taking by speculators. The steep 2.2% fall in the rupee (at its trough) versus the U.S. dollar over the last two days was triggered by the bloodbath in the Argentine peso and the Turkish lira - both emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are high-yielding and have weak balance sheets, eliciting comparisons with the rupee. The deterioration of investors' risk appetite will affect most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  negatively, but in particular the high-yielding ones such as the rupiah and the rupee and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries that have weak balance sheets. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 63.00 (psychological support), then at 62.87 (daily upper Bollinger band standard deviation 2), before 62.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 63.50 (psychological resistance), then at 63.70 (top of monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 27, 2014 20:22 ET (01:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140128ea1s0004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict