\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Export Ban Bullish For Nickel - BoA-ML -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2014\par 07:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia's ban on export of unprocessed ore should be "increasingly bullish" for nickel and prices could stabilize before rallying in 3Q, a key demand period, Bank of America-Merrill Lynch says in a report. "Ore inventories give NPI (nickel pig iron) producers some breathing space, but given the ban on nickel ore exports, we believe China's NPI output growth is set to gradually tail off," the bank says, adding that this should help reduce global market surpluses. It maintains a price target for nickel at $17,000/ton in 4Q. However, for copper, exports of concentrates could continue for three years as long as the shipments meet minimum processing requirements. It says the copper physical market may turn weak in 2H, while prices could remain above $7,500/ton in 1H. The slowing pace of economic growth in China, the world's biggest consumer of industrial metals, is likely to weigh on metal demand, analysts say. LME 3-month copper is at $7,193.50/ton, up $13.50, while nickel is at $14,441/ton, down $44 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] Short covering and bargain hunting helped the Australian dollar to rally from a near-five-month low of 88.42 against the yen in Asian trade on Monday. Further upside in the Aussie/yen cross may encounter resistance at 90.05 (hourly chart) and 90.85 (Friday's high). Spot AUD/JPY was recently at 89.36. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] Safe-haven demand is underpinning gold ahead of a possible default of a trust product in China and Argentina's unexpected devaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Joyce Liu, an investment analyst with Phillip Futures, says in a report. Spot gold rose to an intraday high of $1,279.10/oz in the Asian session. If the current uptrend continues, gold prices could find resistance at $1,294/oz, Liu says. Analysts say weak manufacturing data from China and a pullback in global equity markets is triggering safe-haven demand. "The risk-off rout is gaining momentum as global equity markets saw a sea of red bleeding over from Asia to Europe, and even Wall Street slumped Friday," \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  says. This week, the U.S. Federal Reserve's move on interest rates and its bond-buying program will provide further cues to the market. Spot gold is at $1,271.91/oz, up $2.91 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] Rallies in spot USD/JPY (last at 102.48 versus 102.27 late New York Friday) and NZD/USD (last at 0.8231 from 0.8211) is helping the kiwi/yen cross to rebound strongly from a seven-week low of 83.84--touched this morning on emerging-market concerns. Further upside in spot NZD/JPY may encounter resistance at the 55-day moving average--now at 85.00--and 85.85 (hourly chart resistance). Spot NZD/JPY was recently at 84.36. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Although the sharp drop in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields will undermine the dollar further and recent mixed U.S. data releases have offered the dollar little assistance it is expected to stay firm against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy Mitul Kotecha says. "Notably capital flows from Asian equity markets have increased over recent weeks with Philippines, South Korea and Thailand on track to register outflows for the first month of the year," Kotecha says. "Against this background it is unsurprising that both the South Korean won and Philippine peso are the two worst-performing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year. While we expect a reversal in both the near-term outlook is for further pressure," he says. Spot USD/KRW was recently at 1,083.1 after hitting a four-month high of 1,087.7 this morning. Spot USD/PHP at 45.44 after hitting 45.45--its strongest since Aug. 25, 2010. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian shares recoup some early losses as local funds start bargain hunting. The benchmark KLCI is 0.9% lower at 1787.26 at the midday break, edging up from the day's low of 1777.62. "A firm downside bias should keep the index below the 1800 level," says \uc2 RHB Investment Bank\uc2 , tipping support at 1785. In the broader market, gainers trail losers 74 to 728, while 196 shares remain unchanged. Index heavyweight Malayan Banking (1155.KU) sheds 1.8% to MYR9.48 and \uc2 IHH Healthcare\uc2  (5225.KU) falls 1.3% to MYR3.73, while MISC (3816.KU) adds 1.1% to MYR5.66. (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0513 GMT [Dow Jones] A slowdown in last week's manufacturing data from China was the trigger for a global wave of selling that first hit emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is now taking its toll on stocks. \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Frederic Neumann, co-head of Asia economic research, says the risk is great, but this is "no Lehman moment." A credit crunch in China won't reverberate in the same way, the bank says. It could be bad news for commodities, since China is such a big consumer, but the country has a strong current-account surplus and is still a relatively closed economy, which minimizes the impact of incremental slowing. "A sudden jolt would hurt," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "A gradual slowdown, by contrast, might leave global growth little interrupted." \uc2 HSBC\uc2  forecasts China growth to slow to 7.4% in 2014 from 7.7% a year earlier and adds that even after that slowdown China will still add more to global demand this year than last, equivalent to another two Malaysias. "Expectations veer too far between boom and bust, distracting from calmer deliberations about what a slowing China might mean," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  says. (jake.watts@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR could rally much higher as the Indonesian rupiah becomes the next target for speculative investors looking to profit from the emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rout. Signs of increased volatility for the rupiah are appearing as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell 0.5% Monday--its biggest drop since early January. The rupiah has the potential to fall much more, possibly to 12,390, if USD/IDR ends the day above 12,220 and thereby inside the daily Bollinger uptrend channel--a bullish technical signal for the dollar. On Friday, the Indian rupee--a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that bears similar traits to the rupiah because of their high-yielding nature--tumbled sharply. The rupee and rupiah were both attractive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold at a time when there was an abundance of investment inflows related to U.S. quantitative easing. But as the U.S. Federal Reserve reduces its pace of bond buying, these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been losing their appeal. The main reason the rupiah hasn't weakened much so far is \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  is known to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prevent excessive rupiah weakness in order to avert investor panic. Punters, however, will be placing their bets on the USD/IDR nondeliverable forward contracts, which are beyond the influence of \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 , and in this way reflect better market sentiment for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/IDR is now 12,240 from its Friday close of 12,175. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] The Intercontinental Exchange Dollar Index is likely to consolidate for now after hitting a one-month low of 80.153 on Friday. The daily technical picture is still negative-biased as the five-day moving average is below the 15-day moving average and falling; the daily MACD and stochastic indicators are bearish, while the rate-of-change momentum indicator is declining in negative territory. However, a bullish doji hammer candlestick pattern completed on Friday portends short-term support. A rise above Friday's high of 80.555 may encounter resistance at the 55-day moving average, now at 80.616, and then at 80.919 (hour chart resistance). The spot Dollar Index was recently trading at 80.453, more or less unchanged from 80.458 in late New York trade Friday. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] China does not have to loosen policy in the first quarter as its labor market remains tight, despite signs of slowing economy growth, Nomura economists say in a note. The labour market's demand-supply ratio edged up to 1.10 in the fourth quarter from 1.07 in the third, so for every 100 job seekers there are 110 jobs available, suggesting that the nation's job market remains robust, official data show. Economic growth will likely slow to 7.5% in the first quarter and 7.1% in the second quarter, the economists say, adding that the government is likely to loosen monetary policy in the second quarter by cutting the reserve requirement ratio by 50 basis points to boost growth. (liyan.qi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia bolstered North Sea crude-oil markets in 2013 directly through purchases of North Sea barrels by South Korea and China, and indirectly by diverting the former Soviet Union's crude oil production, also called Urals, towards Asia from Europe, Citi Research says. South Korea and China bought around 100,000 bbl/day of North Sea crude last year via arbitrage last year, and are expected to continue buying this year with three cargoes of Forties crude oil already headed towards South Korea in January. Citi says increasing flows of FSU crude towards the east are tightening the structurally short Northwest European crude market and "the loss of FSU flow west contributes to the ongoing resilience of Brent structure and flat price." Charts show that crude flows from the FSU towards the west have been falling steadily from 2012, while FSU crude going east has been rising steadily. Nymex crude is up 26 cents at $96.90/bbl, Brent is down 15 cents at $107.73/bbl. (eric.yep@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 27 Jan 2014 01:12 EDT  Indonesia Export Ban Bullish For Nickel - BoA-ML -2-\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura Securities says the latest Nikkei weakness is providing the first Japan stock buying opportunity of 2014, noting index futures' dip under the psychologically important 15,000 level. Nomura strategist Hiromichi Tamura attributes the decline to a resumption of the risk-off trade on global stock markets, but adds that it remains to be seen whether the risk-off trade will gain momentum. "As risk sentiment has deteriorated, the risk-off trade could gain momentum if the market decides that the upcoming January 28-29 FOMC decision (on further tapering) pays scant regard to the market's concerns," he says, adding that Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  woes are, to some extent, an isolated risk (the Argentine peso has depreciated some 60% against the US dollar since January 2013). Unless USD/JPY drops below 100 and the yen begins to appreciate again, the underlying bull trend in Japanese equities will likely not change, he says. He notes that the U.S. economic recovery remains intact, and any impact on Japanese corporate earnings is likely to be limited, and adds that Abenomics remains an investment theme. Tamura puts the near term downside for the Nikkei at around 14,500.(bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 27, 2014 01:12 ET (06:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140127ea1r000cm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
