\par\par \b \uc2 'No Panic' in Asia Trade Ahead of Fed: IG -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2013\par 07:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] IG chief market strategist Chris Weston says he's seen "no panic at all" ahead of the Fed's policy meeting later Wednesday, reporting "good, orderly two-way business from clients." Investors aren't expecting a disaster in the U.S. market either, with over two-thirds of new positions on S&P futures being long, Weston adds.(mia.lamar@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0534 GMT [Dow Jones] Currently at a 3-month low of 3.2580 to the U.S. dollar, the Malaysia ringgit could be the strongest candidate amongst Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a post-FOMC rebound rally in the event the U.S. Federal Reserve keeps the pace of its bond buying unchanged. The ringgit has lost 1.6% of its value against the greenback since a week ago, but the daily Bollinger chart suggests weakness in the Malaysia ringgit may be slightly overdone. USD/MYR is now overextended past the upper Bollinger band (standard deviation 2), which indicates that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value has fluctuated beyond the typical amplitude of the last 20 days - the parameter most often used for this charting tool. This technical overshoot is usually followed by a pullback toward the Bollinger band standard deviation 1, which is currently at 3.2380. USD/MYR is now 3.2580 from Tuesday's closing of 3.2470. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0522 GMT [Dow Jones] The spot USD/SGD is trading with a buoyant tone Wednesday on the prospect that the Federal Reserve could begin dialing down its $85 billion-a-month bond-buying program as early as at tonight's meeting and on spillover strength from the spot USD/JPY, which saw the yen weakening 0.37% to as low as 103.04 on speculation that Japan's Prime Minister Shinzo Abe may make a growth-strategy announcement in a speech on Thursday. The technical bias on the spot USD/SGD daily chart is positive as the MACD and slow stochastic indicators are bullish, while the five-day moving average is rising above the 15-day moving average. The overnight rise in spot USD/SGD to a two-month high of 1.2593 has stalled just below the 50% Fibonacci retracement of the decline from the Aug. 22 high of 1.2859 to the Oct. 23 low of 1.2336. As long as support at the Dec. 10 reaction low of 1.2465 is not breached, the spot USD/SGD looks on course to advance further in coming days 1.2659, the 61.8% retracement level, and then to the Sept. 13 minor reaction high of 1.2712. The spot USD/SGD was recently at 1.2583, up from 1.2571 in late New York trade Tuesday.(jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to raise its repo rate on Wednesday to keep the recent surge in food prices from spilling over to other products, a poll shows. Fourteen of the 15 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal expect the RBI to raise its repo rate to 8.0% from 7.75%. The remaining economist predicts that the RBI will leave the rate unchanged. Analysts note that both wholesale and retail prices are rising faster than previously anticipated. "Although most of the inflation pressure is supply driven, we think that the RBI would still want to maintain a hawkish stance to ensure that inflation expectations [which are firmly in double-digit territory as per recent surveys] do not rise further," say analysts from \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . RBI Governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  has already established himself as an inflation hawk by raising rates twice since he took over in September, even though economic growth is at a 10-year low, analysts say. The policy outcome is due at 0530 GMT on Wednesday. (sudeep.jain@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0517 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper falls in Asia but remains comfortably in its recent range. Investors stay on the sidelines, awaiting the outcome of a key U.S. Federal Reserve policy meeting later Wednesday. Investors are looking for clues as to when the Fed may start trimming its commodity-friendly stimulus program. Standard Bank says it has raised its 2013 forecasts for China demand for copper and aluminum as physical and financial demand have exceeded expectations, which provides "a firmer footing" for next year. Credit conditions remain tight in China in 2H as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrains from pumping money into the money market - the latest evidence coming from Wednesday's money-market rates, which surged to their highest levels since June. LME three-month copper is down 0.5% at $7,242.50/ton. (yue.li@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0500 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian shares recoup some of their early losses but remain weak at the midday break. The benchmark KLCI is down 0.1% at 1848.86. "Local funds started selective stock picking, but the overall caution ahead of the (U.S.) Federal Reserve's policy decision will keep the index subdued," says a dealer, tipping immediate support at 1840. In the broader market, gainers trail laggards 234 to 364, while 304 shares remain unchanged. Among the index shares, \uc2 Petronas Gas\uc2  (6033.KU) leads gains with a 2.1% rise to MYR23.68 and heavyweight Malayan Banking (1155.KU) adds 0.2% to MYR10.04, while \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) falls 0.7% to MYR6.78 and \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 .(1961.KU) sheds 0.9% to MYR5.76. (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] The rupee is slightly stronger Wednesday before the 0530 GMT policy decision announcement by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 . The odds that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might raise interest rates became stronger after higher-than-expected November inflation data released Monday. The rupee is the outperformer in South Asia this week as all other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region depreciate against the dollar ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve policy decision later today. India's benchmark inflation rate was 7.5% in November versus expectations for 7.0% -- raising the possibility that the RBI could hike interest rates again, which would make it a third time in four months. A rate hike could lift the rupee due to the larger interest rate differential which implies rupee-denominated assets would yield more attractive returns than dollar-denominated ones. USD/INR is now 61.89 from Tuesday's closing of 62.01. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 closes down 1.1% at 4675.878 as the market remains soft due to strength in the NZD/AUD seeing Australian funds selling to take \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  profits, says Macquarie Private Wealth Associate Director Rob Gwyther. He adds there is also little else in the market to drive buying and this was resulting in light volumes exacerbating moves. Large-cap stock Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) closes down 2.2% at NZ$8.35, \uc2 Contact Energy\uc2  was down 2.4% at NZ$4.87 and Skycity (SKC.NZ) closes down 1.8% to NZ$3.75. Summerset (SUM.NZ) is the one standout performer, ending up 1.3% at NZ$3.23 after announcing it was purchasing two Christchurch sites on which to build villages. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Glenn Stevens\uc2  is keeping his options open. He won't rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower the Australian dollar, cutting interest rates further or introducing new regulatory tools to slow lending to keep house prices in check, he tells a parliamentary economics committee on Wednesday. Policy adjustments will be made if the conditions warrant, Stevens says. Stevens says "watch this space," hinting the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  might soon publish research into new clamps on home lending. Prices are rising because of record-low interest rates. "On occasion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be quite useful. It still is," Stevens says. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone can't revive a flagging economy, he says and calls on other policy makers to build confidence in the economy. (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD has traded sideways over the course of the session, with the market focus all on the FOMC decision later in the global day and the potential for it to announce it will start to taper its bond-buying program, says Chris Hunter, corporate dealing manager at \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions. "Tomorrow morning is going to provide the direction if not for the next quarter, then for the rest of the year as to what the market is going to do with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." The pair is at 0.8264 vs 0.8271 early this morning. Hunter adds that NZ 3Q GDP, due Thursday, is expected to be a strong number and "this may reduce the impact of a U.S. dollar positive announcement by the Fed or it might just boost the New Zealand dollar higher." He puts support ahead of the FOMC at 0.8235, with resistance at 0.8285. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0307 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is steady after central parity is moved a fraction only. The pair is at 6.0715 vs 6.0710 Tuesday close after the fixing is set at 6.1105 vs 6.1108 Tuesday. Traders said the yuan will likely have strong support at 6.0700. "I don't expect the [U.S.] Federal Reserve to surprise the market with a tapering decision and the yuan is unlikely to get any incentive from the U.S. side," a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader says. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are at 6.1290/6.1320 from 6.1279/6.1322 late Tuesday. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar's routing of the Indonesia rupiah continues after yet another near five-year record was hit on Tuesday. The rupiah is back at Tuesday's 12,150 low against the greenback as traders, emboldened by a lack of willingness by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against rupiah weakness, bought the dollar on a bet that the U.S. Federal Reserve might decide to reduce bond-buying at its policy meeting today. The rupiah is widely seen as one of the more vulnerable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event the Fed decides to cut back on bond purchases - which could trigger foreign outflows that will further weaken the rupiah. The Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been conspicuously absent in the spot market except for recent verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to talk the dollar down. Even the Monday announcement of raising foreign-investment limits in certain sectors of the economy, such as airports and railways, didn't help the rupiah to recoup losses. USD/IDR is now at 12,150, against Tuesday's closing of 12,110. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 18, 2013 01:59 ET (06:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131218e9ci000j3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
