\par\par \b \uc2 Southeast Asian Markets End Week Slightly Higher; Rupiah Pushes 4.5-Year Low\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jake Maxwell Watts   \par \uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2013\par 11:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Southeast Asian markets ended Friday mostly higher, with Thailand and Indonesia making strong gains in the last part of the day, while Indonesia's rupiah retreated from a 4.5-year low against the U.S. dollar on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thailand's SET closed up 0.9%, recovering from a 0.5% fall during morning trading. Indonesia's JSX closed up 0.5%, while the Philippines' PSE Composite gained 0.6%--its fourth straight day of gains after over a week of declines triggered by the landfall of Typhoon Haiyan on Nov. 8.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Vietnam's benchmark closed flat, Malaysia's KLCI added 0.3% and Singapore's FTSE Straits Times bucked the trend with a fall of 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Central bankers in both the Philippines and Indonesia attempted to reassure investors Friday. Governor Amando Tetangco in the Philippines said that the impact of Typhoon Haiyan on the economy would be manageable and domestic growth should hit targets of this year and next without \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The government is targeting 6.5%-7.5% growth in 2014.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia governor Agus Martowardojo said that the rupiah, which is hovering near a 4.5%-year low of 12,000 against the U.S. dollar, reflected economic fundamentals despite heavy falls this year of almost 20%. He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would prioritize stabilizing the economy rather than boosting growth. There was some speculation among traders that \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday to keep the rupiah from breaching the 12,000 mark.\par \par \uc2 Among other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, Malaysia's ringgit strengthened 0.3% and Thailand's baht lost 0.1% against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Thai real-estate developer Central Pattana Public Co., a volatile large-cap stock, added 2.4%, while telecoms provider Total Access Communications Public Co.--also known as DTAC--ended up 5%.\par \par \uc2 Philippine stocks continued to recover from a blip in investor sentiment following the typhoon. Telecoms provider \uc2 Philippine Long Distance Telephone Co\uc2 . added 2.1%, while property investor \uc2 SM Investments Corporation\uc2  gained 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 In Malaysia, conglomerate \uc2 Genting Bhd\uc2 ., which has interests in casinos, power, plantations and other businesses, lost 1.4% a day after it reported third-quarter results that Kenanga Research said came in below expectations. Genting said its core net profit for the nine months to September was 1.29 billion ringgit (US$400 million). The group's earnings are expected to be better in the fourth quarter of 2013 as it is entering the busy festive year-end season, Kenanga said in a note, maintaining its "outperform" rating.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jake Maxwell Watts at jake.watts@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 29, 2013 05:54 ET (10:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131129e9bt000rs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
