\par\par \b \uc2 Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Eyed Near 12,000 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,039 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2013\par 03:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] As the rupiah lingers near its 4.5-year low of IDR12,000 against the dollar, traders are likely wary of the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepping in to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide past the major psychological trading barrier. USD/IDR's spike to 12,000 on Thursday likely prompted authorities to step into the market and sell U.S. dollars to stem the rupiah's fall, dealers said. The rupiah hit a 4.5-year trough despite suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the 11,900 level onwards. The Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is notoriously low on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and thus has less 'ammunition' to spare for defending its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  via direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This may be a reason why USD/IDR generally heads higher even while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The daily USD/IDR chart does however suggest a slight pullback may be due, now that the dollar has extended significantly past the top of the upper Bollinger band (standard deviation 2), which is a sign that the dollar may be overbought by speculators. USD/IDR is now 11,975. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is slightly higher, mostly lifted by buying from foreign investors, say two local traders. The pair is at 29.612, slightly higher than 29.600 traded before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, which lifted it to 29.680 at the close of onshore trading session, traders say. They note some foreign investors bought aggressively soon after the open but the buying abated. "The greenback was also lifted by buying from some importers, possibly related to military settlements," a trader adds. They tip the pair to trade in a 29.580-29.620 band for the session. The Taiex is up 0.4%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] BMD CPO futures are likely to stay in recent range as investors await key market outlooks at industry meetings in Indonesia. A Kuala Lumpur-based broker says a surge above MYR2,670/ton resistance level may spark fresh buying interest, potentially lifting values toward crucial level of MYR2,700/ton. Leading analyst Dorab Mistry is scheduled to present market forecast around 0800 GMT and is widely expected to dish out bullish CPO production and biodiesel demand forecasts, the broker says. Benchmark February CPO ended up 0.6% at MYR2,656/ton Thursday. (shie-lynn.lim@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] The South Korea won rises to a 5-year high of 10.3255 versus the Japanese yen as the USD/KRW slips Friday, falling back into a well-defined technical range of 1,058-1,063. Exporters' repatriation of earnings and equity inflows from foreigners helped the won to recover from initial losses yesterday. The value of the South Korea won versus the Japanese yen is watched by traders due to the possibility that \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2  could step in to weaken the won in order to ensure the relative price competitiveness of South Korean exports. If the BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market, USD/KRW might head back up to the 20-day Bollinger mid resistance line at 1,063 from its current level of 1,059.4. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0143 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil futures are steady in early Asian trade Friday but the premium of ICE Brent crude over Nymex WTI remains wide as the two oil benchmarks continue to diverge. "The spread remains above $19/bbl, a differential not seen since early May and well above the five year November average of $7/bbl," ANZ Research says in a note. It says news of the oil workers union moving towards a resolution to reopen Libyan oil terminals has caused some softness in Brent, while WTI remains under pressure on larger-than-expected increases in U.S. oil inventories. "Further detail from this week's Dept. of Energy report included the largest weekly output increase, of 45,000 barrels, in 25 years," ANZ says. Next week, markets will track major economic data releases for cues--including U.S. non-farm payrolls data. Nymex is down 5 cents at $92.25/bbl, Brent crude is up 3 cents at $110.89/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] Data out of Japan this morning (unemployment, CPI and industrial production) point to an overall picture of a weak yen amid moderate economic recovery, but no indications yet that domestic demand is strong enough to reach and sustain the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's 2.0% inflation target, says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 . "As such, the pressure will only increase for further quantitative easing ahead given there is no sign of structural reform to drive growth," the bank says. More QE from the BOJ will reduce the allure of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Against the dollar, the yen was recently at 102.42 after hitting a six-month low of 102.44 this morning. Against the euro, it was last at 139.35 after falling to a five-year low of 139.41. (jerry.tan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0124 GMT [Dow Jones] Likely to be another dull session in the spot the USD/CNY market, which has barely budged more than 10-pips the whole week, as the U.S. market holiday Thursday reduced volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The PBOC central parity rate is expected to hover around its recent levels, though the slightly firmer USD index overnight could raise the possibility of a higher central parity rate. China's recent unilateral declaration of an air defence zone over disputed islands, which has been tested by the U.S. and Japan, has not affected investors' sentiment much. One of the prevailing short-term trading themes in the USD/CNY is for a stronger yuan benchmark exchange rate towards end-December, as the PBOC typically sets the parity rate higher for the yuan at year-end. The USD/CNY closed Thursday at 6.0925. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 28, 2013 21:34 ET (02:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131129e9bt0001m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
