\par\par \b \uc2 PREVIEW-Indonesia's trade gap to narrow a bit, but pressure still on rupiah\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rieka Rahadiana   \par \uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 November 2013\par 09:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Nov 29 (Reuters) - Indonesia is expected to post a slightly narrower trade deficit in October, as aggressive rate rises start to subdue demand for imports, while November's annual inflation may pick up on high food prices, a Reuters poll showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The poll's median projection was for the trade deficit in Southeast Asia's largest economy to come in at $650 million, slightly less than the $660 million gap of the previous month.\par \par \uc2 Imports in October were forecast to fall 8.11 percent year-on-year, from September's 0.77 percent rise, as a weakening exchange rate crimped demand, while higher interest rates also tempered borrowing and affected importers.\par \par \uc2 Exports showed some signs of improvement bolstered by a recovery in commodity prices, but stayed weak. October exports were estimated to fall 2.75 percent from a year earlier, against a drop of 6.85 percent the previous month.\par \par \uc2 "Strong domestic consumption has sustained, while export growth is unlikely to see a strong boost until 2014 - and that is also dependent on how well the global economy picks up," said Gundy Cahyadi, economist at DBS in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia has been struggling to stabilise its external balance, as a large current-account deficit and weak structural reforms have turned foreign investors bearish.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 , under pressure from the rupiah's steep fall, has raised its benchmark reference rate a total of 175 basis points since June to ease concerns over the country's vulnerability to capital outflows ahead of the Federal Reserve's expected tapering in monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 The poll of 14 analysts projected that November's rate of inflation quickened to 8.48 percent, versus October's 8.32 percent, on stubbornly high prices for foodstuff and pass-through costs from a depreciation in the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 On a monthly basis, the consumer price index was seen up 0.20 percent, higher than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forecast of 0.05-0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 MORE RATE HIKES SEEN\par \par \uc2 Weakness in trade and further declines in the rupiah could give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more reason to increase the benchmark reference rate at its December policy meeting or after.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will continue to raise the benchmark rate until there are imminent signs of optimism that the current-account deficit eases to below 3 percent of GDP. Thus, we see BI lifting the rate up to 8.5 percent in Q1-2014," said Arga Samudro, economist at \uc2 Bahana Securities\uc2  in Jakarta.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah hit its support level of 12,000 per dollar on Thursday due to increasing dollar demand from Indonesian firms. Asia's worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost nearly 20 percent to the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, Governor of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Agus Martowardojo said the bank will not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and considers the recent slide temporary.\par \par \uc2 The finance ministry estimates the current-account deficit to narrow in the fourth quarter to $7 billion, compared with $8.4 billion in the previous quarter.\par \par \uc2 In April-June, the current-account deficit was $9.9 billion or the equivalent of 4.4 percent of GDP.\par \par \uc2 For the latest estimates on Indonesia economic data, please see. For the Reuters poll, click on\par \par \uc2 (Editing by \uc2 Jacqueline Wong\uc2 )\par \par \uc