\par\par \b \uc2 USD/Asia Buoyant, Yen Weakness Eyed -- Asia Daily  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 1,945 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2013\par 02:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY   6.0900-6.1000   6.0802-6.1030 \par USD/TWD   29.560-29.650   29.500-29.710 \par USD/KRW    1,063-1,068     1,060-1,070 \par USD/SGD   1.2510-1.2568   1.2500-1.2598 \par USD/MYR   3.2140-3.2320   3.2000-3.2370 \par USD/THB    32.00-32.20     31.80-32.39 \par USD/PHP    43.62-43.80     43.33-43.88 \par USD/IDR   11,800-11,900   11,640-12,000 \par USD/INR    62.00-62.72     61.47-63.00 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY--consolidation. Spot USD/CNY is expected to be stable once again, though the slightly firmer USD index overnight could raise the possibility of a higher PBOC USD/CNY central parity rate. The recently less volatile spot market continues to flatline; the dollar has only fluctuated within a 10-pip range this entire week. The territorial disputes and China's recent unilateral air defence zone declaration has seemingly not affected investors' sentiment so far. There are some punters in the NDF market who have placed bets for a stronger yuan benchmark exchange rate towards end-December, as the PBOC typically sets the parity rate higher for the yuan at year-end. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 6.0900 (psychological support), then at 6.0894 (entrance of Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.0802 (record low). Immediate resistance is at 6.1000 (psychological resistance), before 6.1030 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD--possible upside bias. USD/TWD is creeping higher into the Bollinger uptrend channel, which could give the dollar additional technical momentum to test the top of the daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone that currently caps at 29.710. If this resistance level holds, consolidation within the Cloud range of 29.500-29.710 will likely continue. The USD index slipped firmed up overnight as USD/JPY rallied past the 102.00 psychological barrier; yen weakening could have an additional proxy effect on Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The NDF market is showing a tinge of bullishness for the greenback, as the benchmark 1-month NDF discount continues to shrink. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 29.560 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 29.500 (psychological support and base of Ichimoku Cloud), before 29.460 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 29.650 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 29.710 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone).\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW--consolidation higher. USD/KRW has busted out of the previously well-confined range 1,058-1,063, which had been intact for 5 days, and now looks likely to bob in a higher range of 1,063-1,068. The dollar gained overnight on an improvement in U.S. economic data, including weekly jobless claims, that might make the Federal Reserve consider a December reduction in bond-buying. The South Korea won may also be under the influence of the weakening Japanese yen, which has dropped past the 102.00 psychological barrier, thus spurring more yen-selling in the market. Further yen weakness may trigger a response from \uc2 the Bank of Korea\uc2 , which keeps a close watch on the yen/won cross exchange rate to ensure the relative price competitiveness of South Korean exports. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,063 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 1,060 (psychological support), before 1,058 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1,068 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel and Ichimoku Cloud resistance), then at 1,070 (psychological resistance), before 1,072 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/SGD--upside bias. The Singapore dollar looks likely to fall against the greenback as the charts suggest further USD/SGD upside is likely toward the top of the daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone at 1.2598. USD/SGD got a boost from the Japanese yen weakening past the 102.00 psychological barrier; the yen has a proxy effect on most Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it makes large movements. The USD/SGD uptrend carries on for a fifth day helped by the USD index rising slightly overnight. Data released Tuesday showed Singapore's October industrial production rose 8.0% on year, which was short of expectations for a rise of 9.3%. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2510 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2500 (psychological support), before 1.2472 (20-day Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is at 1.2568 (top of weekly Ichimoku Cloud resistance zone), then at 1.2598 (top of daily Ichimoku Cloud), before 1.2600 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR--upside bias. USD/MYR may attempt to break through the top of the daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance at 3.2370, which would lend even more bullishness to the dollar. The dollar got a boost overnight from healthy U.S. economic data, including a slightly lower weekly unemployment claims report, which could reignite speculation that the U.S. Federal Reserve might taper its bond-buying program earlier. The USD/MYR daily Bollinger uptrend channel, which has been supporting the dollar's rally since Friday, will remain in play as long as USD/MYR stays above 3.2140 by the end of the day. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2140 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 3.2000 (psychological barrier), before 3.1980 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud). Immediate resistance is at 3.2320 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 3.2370 (top of Ichimoku Cloud resistance), before 3.2500 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB--upside bias. The baht has barely reacted to the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's surprise 25-basis point benchmark interest rate cut Wednesday, though the stock market rallied 1.1%. The main theme that is keeping the baht weak continues to be the ongoing anti-government protests in Thailand. The BOT caught markets off-guard, cutting the benchmark interest rate to 2.25% from 2.50%, citing low inflation as a factor behind the decision. Last week, the National Economic and Social Development Board revised down its full-year growth projection to 3.0% from 3.8%-4.3%. Since the anti-government protests began, the baht has lost about 3.1% of its value through November and could weaken further as USD/THB aims for the top of the weekly Bollinger uptrend channel at 32.39. There are no signs that embattled Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra intends to give in to protestors' demands that she resign. Thailand's trade data for October released Tuesday disappointed as exports fell 0.7% and imports shrank 5.4%. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.00 (psychological support), then at 31.80 (psychological support), before 31.67 (Ichimoku Cloud support zone). Immediate resistance is at 32.20 (psychological resistance), then at 32.39 (top of weekly Bollinger uptrend channel), before 32.48 (Sept. 6 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP--consolidation. USD/PHP is likely to be well-supported by the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 43.62, but is also well-capped by the top of the channel which coincides with the Ichimoku Cloud ceiling at 43.88. The Philippines will release its Q3 gross domestic product report at 0200 GMT, which may not have a very strong impact on the peso as the market focuses on predominant dollar strength across Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Economists polled by the Wall Street Journal expect growth of 7.1% for the quarter. Improvements in U.S. weekly jobless claims data released overnight may have given the USD a boost, as it raises the possibility that job growth is accelerating and therefore the Federal Reserve has less need to keep monetary stimulus at full bore. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 43.62 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 43.33 (base of daily Ichimoku Cloud). Immediate resistance is likely at 43.80 (psychological resistance), then at 43.88 (top of Ichimoku Cloud and top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 44.00 (psychological barrier).\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR--mild pullback. USD/IDR shot higher late Wednesday to a new 4.5-year peak of 11,880, driven by broad dollar bullishness. The dollar may pullback slightly Thursday as the chart suggests an overextension of the rally that could motivate some profit-taking by speculators who are long the dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been mentioned to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to support the rupiah yet, but they are likely to be present in the market soon, especially as the 12,000 major psychological barrier approaches. Apart from improvements in U.S. jobs data that raises the likelihood of the Federal Reserve tapering quantitative easing, credit ratings agency Fitch is downbeat on the Indonesian economy, lowering its growth forecast for next year's Gross Domestic Product to 5.3% from 5.5% in 2013. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 11,800 (psychological support), then at 11,640 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 11,500 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 11,900 (psychological resistance), before 12,000 (major psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 27 Nov 2013 20:02 EDT  USD/Asia Buoyant, Yen Weakness Eyed -- Asia Daily -2-\par \par \uc2 USD/INR--consolidation. Easing oil prices continue to help the India rupee recover, but technical support for the dollar is now likely to keep USD/INR well-supported. The dollar, which has dipped nearly to the 20-day Bollinger mid support and is also near the base of the Ichimoku Cloud - another technical support - could stabilize for now. The USD/INR daily chart suggests a range of 62.00-62.72 with a possibility of a rebound higher as the greenback ekes out gains overnight against the majors. The drop in oil prices is seen as a boon to the India economy and helpful in reducing the current account deficit, thus beneficial to the rupee. But consistent demand for the U.S. dollar from onshore oil importers may keep USD/INR supported on dips. The deputy governor of the Reserve Bank of India on Monday said that a 'big chunk' of oil importers are back in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market buying dollars, as the government's scheme to allow them to buy USD directly from the RBI gets gradually removed. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 62.00 (20-day Bollinger mid support and psychological support), then at 61.94 (base of Ichimoku Cloud support), before 61.47 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 62.72 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 63.00 (psychological trading barrier), before 63.34 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 27, 2013 20:02 ET (01:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131128e9bs0003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
