\par\par \b \uc2 USD/Asia Firm Before U.S. Non-Farm Payrolls -- Asia Daily  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 1,671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2013\par 02:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY   6.0900-6.1000   6.0800-6.1109 \par USD/TWD   29.320-29.500   29.200-29.570 \par USD/KRW    1,060-1,066     1,056-1,070 \par USD/SGD   1.2422-1.2472   1.2400-1.2500 \par USD/MYR   3.1670-3.1800   3.1500-3.2000 \par USD/THB    31.00-31.14     30.80-31.20 \par USD/PHP    43.00-43.14     42.80-43.25 \par USD/IDR   11,300-11,400   11,170-11,500 \par USD/INR    61.50-62.00     61.31-62.50 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY--consolidation with downside bias. The dollar consolidated overnight as traders seemingly stay on the sidelines until the U.S. non-farm payrolls report is released Tuesday. USD/CNY could therefore stabilize as well, though there is a chance for more yuan appreciation as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lately been allowing the yuan to strengthen in the open market as China attempts to shift its economy toward a more consumption-based one. The dollar may hover around its recent record low of 6.0915 until the U.S. non-farm payrolls data--a likely source of market volatility--is out of the way. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 6.0900 (psychological support), then at 6.0800 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 6.1000 (psychological resistance), then at 6.1109 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD--consolidation with downside bias. A touch of dollar strength appears, as the market readjusts positioning ahead of this week's data highlight--the U.S. non-farm payrolls for September. USD/TWD is bouncing off the base of the Bollinger downtrend channel and looking for the channel ceiling at 29.570. As long as the pair stays below this key resistance mark by the end of the day, the downside bias for USD/TWD remains. The possibility of a strong employment outlook could alter market expectations for a dovish Federal Reserve, which explains why traders have been trimming dollar-shorts this week. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 29.320 (weekly Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 29.200 (psychological support), before 29.000 (major psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 29.500 (psychological resistance), then at 29.570 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel and weekly Ichimoku Cloud resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW--consolidation with downside bias. USD/KRW has been rocksteady towards the 1,060 psychological support, likely due to Bank of Korea \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeping won strength at bay, reckons a trader. But the possibility of a strong U.S. non-farm payrolls report, due later today, is also keeping the dollar up broadly. But the bearish bias for USD/KRW will remain as long as the pair stays under 1,066--the top of the Bollinger downtrend channel. Foreign investors' inflows into equities and fixed income assets that have recently been driving won gains will likely resume after the volatility of the U.S. non-farm payrolls ebbs. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,060 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel and psychological support), before 1,056 (base of weekly Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1,066 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,070 (psychological resistance), before 1,072 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/SGD--consolidation higher. USD/SGD is now free of the bearish bias cast by the Bollinger downtrend channel, after the greenback rose overnight, and could now consolidate within a slightly higher range. The dollar firmed broadly overnight, rising above the top of the Bollinger downtrend channel at 1.2422 as traders reduced dollar-shorts, preparing for the possibility of a strong U.S. non-farm payrolls number due Tuesday. USD/SGD could range within 1.2422-1.2472 for now, as the market awaits the keenly-eyed data due later in the global day. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2422 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1.2400 (psychological support), before 1.2380 (base of weekly Ichimoku Cloud support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2472 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 1.2500 (psychological resistance), before 1.2523 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR--consolidation higher. USD/MYR has negated its previous bias, now trading above the Bollinger downtrend channel after a bout of USD-short covering this week fuelled the pair higher. The possibility of an upbeat U.S. non-farm payrolls report is prompting traders to reduce bets that the Federal Reserve will keep monetary stimulus at full steam for the rest of the year. USD/MYR could for now consolidate within a higher range of 3.1670-3.1940, capped by the 20-day Bollinger mid resistance line. If this level is breached, traders will likely be gunning for the stoploss orders waiting above the major psychological resistance at 3.2000, which could trigger a bullish technical signal for the greenback. The Malaysia government will release its budget statement this Friday, which could help to buffer the ringgit if it addresses the massive fiscal deficit and the heavy reliance on foreign debt--issues that led to ratings agencies warning on Malaysia's sovereign credit rating. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.1670 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 3.1500 (psychological support), before 3.1400 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 3.1800 (psychological resistance), then at 3.1940 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance), before 3.2000 (major psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB--consolidation higher. USD/THB is on the brink of exiting the Bollinger downtrend channel on the daily charts, as the greenback remains firm just before the U.S. non-farm payrolls report for September is released. The possibility of a strong employment report has led to trimming of dollar-shorts this week. If USD/THB ends Tuesday above 31.14 and therefore out of the bearish channel, it could be headed for a higher consolidation zone, mimicking price action in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But if the Bollinger downtrend channel resistance holds, expect further downside for the rest of the week. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 31.00 (psychological support), then at 30.80 (psychological support), before 30.74 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 31.14 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 31.20 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP--consolidation higher. The dollar is expected to stay firm as the market awaits the release of U.S. non-farm payrolls for September later today. USD/PHP may attempt to exit the daily Bollinger downtrend channel that currently caps at 43.14, and could then consolidate in a higher range. Traders appear to have priced in the possibility of a stronger U.S. employment report that could signal to the Federal Reserve that monetary stimulus can be reduced. As the Philippines remittance season begins, when millions of overseas workers remit larger-than-usual amounts to their families ahead of the year-end holidays, the extra demand for the peso could help support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 43.00 (psychological support), then at 42.80 (psychological support), before 42.62 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 43.14 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 43.25 (200-week moving average), before 43.50 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR--consolidation. USD/IDR is likely to stay firm as traders are mildly bullish the dollar ahead of the release of U.S. non-farm payrolls later today. Traders are also slightly wary that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem rupiah strength, in order to keep the country's exports attractively priced. The rupiah is still the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia this year, down 15.1% to date, mainly due to current account woes and weakening export performance affecting the trade balance as well. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 11,300 (psychological support), then at 11,170 (20-day Bollinger mid support), before 11,000 (major psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 11,400 (psychological resistance), then at 11,500 (psychological resistance), before 11,530 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/INR--consolidation. USD/INR may hover just above the entrance to the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, as traders shy from selling the dollar ahead of U.S. non-farm payrolls due later today. The market has been reducing dollar-shorts this week, hedging for a possibly strong U.S. employment number that could alter expectations that the Federal Reserve will keep up its pace of quantitative easing for the rest of the year. If however, USD/INR ends Tuesday beneath the 61.31 entrance of the downtrend channel, downside bias for the dollar could return. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 61.31 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 61.00 (psychological support), before 60.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 62.00 (psychological resistance), then at 62.50 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 21, 2013 20:32 ET (00:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131022e9am0009v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
