\par\par \b \uc2 Rupiah Still Worst Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  This Year -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2013\par 06:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] The Indonesia rupiah is the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia this year, down 15.2% to date, even though the U.S. dollar is generally a tad softer after the debt ceiling was raised. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears unwilling to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  much, opting to let market forces dictate the rupiah's value. The Indonesia governemnt may be accepting of the weaker rupiah for now, which makes the country's exports seem more attractive. The dollar slipped across the board following the deal to end the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  shutdown and to raise the debt ceiling, removing one big uncertainty for financial markets. The USD/IDR has a slightly bearish tone after dropping out of the Bollinger uptrend channel last week, which implies the dollar could next be headed for the 20-day Bollinger mid at 11,125 due to technical mean reversion on the charts. The USD/IDR is now 11,350. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are just mildly stronger after the U.S. House voted to raise the debt ceiling, at least until February. The vibe in the market suggests that this result was already priced in, with some participants commenting that the consequences of not passing the bill would have been too grave to not pass it - hence the result was already expected. The greenback has slipped a tad as positive risk sentiment from the equities market helps the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rally slightly. Looking ahead, a slew of U.S. data including non-farm payrolls, that had been postponed due to the government shutdown, will likely be released over the next few days. The data may affirm expectations that incoming Federal Reserve chief Janet Yellen will keep quantitative easing in place for longer. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil futures are likely to remain rangebound before markets open in Europe and the U.S., as investors are still cautious over developments in the U.S. amid a last-minute deal in Washington to reopen the federal government and avoid a debt crisis. "I think \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have mostly been priced in and Asian investors want to see how markets perform later in Europe and the U.S. before taking positions," says Kaname Gokon, deputy general manager of research at Tokyo-based commodity brokerage \uc2 Okato Shoji Co\uc2 . He adds oil prices are nonetheless supported by China and South Korean demand ahead of winter; he tips Nymex to move in the $101-$103/bbl range and Brent to move in the $109-$112/bbl range. Nymex crude is down 7 cents at $102.22/bbl, Brent crude is up 1 cent at $110.60/bbl. (huileng.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0357 GMT [Dow Jones] The last-minute deal to temporarily extend \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  borrowing authority allows the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  to reopen and avoid a debt default. But the partial shutdown over the past three weeks could shave about 0.5% from fourth-quarter GDP--potentially extending bond buying and sparking discussion about the need for more, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  economist Elliot Clarke says. "It will be some time before we are able to get a clear read on the labor market postshutdown but a logical expectation given recent events and the lack of a long-term solution is that we will see soft employment growth through the remainder of 2013 and into 2014," Mr. Clarke says. "This trend looks set to restrict consumption growth to a subpar pace with any bursts of credit-supported durables growth offset by weakness elsewhere. Soft employment growth also poses a risk to the housing market already weakened by the sizeable rise in mortgage rates through 2013." World-wide equities are likely to be relatively strong and the U.S. dollar could weaken against growth-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar, Mr. Elliot says. (david.rogers1@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0258 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD eases lightly after a potentially crippling U.S. debt default was averted when Congress passed legislation to end a two-week political battle. "We never expected them to not have a deal but it's almost buy the rumor, sell the fact," says Tim Kelleher, ASB Bank's head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Institutional Sales. "We are seeing a bit of U.S. dollar strength but it's just kicking the can down the road to January and February," he says. The pair is at 0.8424 and Kelleher says it has support at 0.8375 and resistance around 0.8450. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnamese banks led by \uc2 Vietcombank\uc2  (VCB.VH) raise their USD/VND rates to 21,100-21,150 Thursday from 21,080-21,136 Wednesday due to low liquidity in the interbank market, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say. "Some big banks have raised their rates after state media cited \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials as saying they are buying the greenback in the domestic market to replenish the state coffer after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a large amount of dollars to import gold for local sales in recent months," says a dealer with a joint stock commercial bank in Hanoi. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it has imported 62.57 metric tons of gold since March. (phammuoi.nguyen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is lower, reflecting the USD's weakness against most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  passed a debt bill, says a local trader. The pair is at 29.397, lower than the 29.410 level it was at before the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, which lifted it to 29.460 at the close of onshore trading session, traders says. A trader says continued foreign fund inflows to local stocks (the Taiex is up 0.5%) are still weighing on the pair, and expects the pair to trade in a 29.350-29.420 band for the session. Taiwan government bonds track gains in U.S. Treasurys Wednesday, with the most-traded five-year bond yield falling to 1.1375% vs 1.1560% late Wednesday; a trader says the yield is still likely to fall below 1.1300% if the U.S. benchmark 10-year bond yield breaks below 2.60%. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] As the greenback weakens due to investors selling the dollar for risk assets upon the deal to end the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  shutdown and to raise the debt ceiling, a potential bearish signal is forming on the weekly USD/SGD chart. The USD/SGD already exhibits a bearish bias on the daily charts, entrenched inside the Bollinger downtrend channel, but a Friday close below 1.2383 - the base of the weekly Ichimoku Cloud - would be an additionally bearish signal for the USD/SGD that could point the pair to the May low of 1.2261. The USD/SGD is now 1.2433. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Richard Harris, CEO of Port Shelter Investment Management in Hong Kong, isn't among those concerned that a last-minute U.S. debt deal raises the government's borrowing limit only through February. He expects the issue will be resolved with much less drama next time around as Congress avoids another bruising political saga. "They kicked the can down the road but it's now a pretty full can," Harris says. "I think this particular issue has probably lost its force." (mia.lamar@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is lower early Thursday on news the U.S. is set to forge a final deal on the debt ceiling and budget to avoid a default and reopen the government. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair slipped to 43.06 after settling at 43.19 late Wednesday. A local bank trader says, however, further losses by the USD/PHP may be limited on concerns of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  once the dollar approaches 43.00. Volume is relatively heavy. Traders expect the USD/PHP to stay within the 43.00-43.50 near-term trading range. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] China's yuan is nearly flat early Thursday amid tight liquidity and small fluctuation in the dollar overnight. The dollar yuan is last at 6.0991 roughly at its Wednesday close of 6.0995, even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the pair at 6.1431, slightly up from 6.1408 Wednesday. The WSJ Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 72.823 late Wednesday in New York--slightly higher than 72.773 late Tuesday. "Because of the big upward movement in the yuan in recent sessions, liquidity is a bit tight. Traders are expecting the yuan to take back some gains," says a Shanghai-based trader with a local bank. "Overall the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be pretty stable as the dollar hasn't moved much, even with the debt ceiling deadline drawing near," he says. Offshore, one-year dollar/yuan nondeliverable forward contracts are down at 6.1555/6.1575, from 6.1475/6.1510 late Wednesday. (chao.deng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Preview: Hong Kong's July-September jobless rate is likely steady at 3.3% and unchanged from June-August, according to the median forecast of four economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. "Economic activities from retails to trade have picked up nicely in recent months on the back of Chinese recovery lending a strong support to Hong Kong's employment," ANZ senior economist Raymond Yeung says. The government is scheduled to release the data at 0830 GMT on Thursday. (chester.yung@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 17, 2013 00:55 ET (04:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131017e9ah000lv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
