\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: EUR/USD Approaching Formidable Technical Support At 1.3143.\b0\par\par\uc2 1,608 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2013\par 08:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0653 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The 1.3143 mark could prove to be a strong floor for EUR/USD even as the market awaits an ECB decision on interest rates. The base of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel meets the Ichimoku Cloud at 1.3143 making this support more significant. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been drifting lower on Thursday partly owing to the influence of upcoming U.S. jobs data on Thursday and Friday which may print on the strong side and thereby signal a likely Fed tapering of QE this month. The ECB is widely expected to keep its main policy rate unchanged at a record low of 0.5%. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Further upside for the AUD/USD pair could be less likely if the pair ends Thursday below 0.9148--the base of the Bollinger uptrend channel. The rebound of the greenback comes ahead of U.S. ADP jobs data, which is the highlight for today. A global daily close out of the uptrend channel could see the Aussie drop further to 0.9059, where the 20-day Bollinger mid support lies. A positive surprise in the ADP data could indicate a strong non-farm payrolls number Friday, which would further fuel market speculation that the Fed will decide to taper QE3 at this month's FOMC meeting. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0608 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/JPY is up as \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields rise above 2.9%, a senior dealer at a large Japanese trust bank says. "Asian central banks may be selling Treasuries to buy their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he adds. The USD/JPY is at 99.85 after hitting a six-week high of 99.99 from an earlier low of 99.63. Still the pair is capped by options-related selling and orders from Japanese exporters, some Tokyo dealers say. Once it rises above 100 it may continue on to 100.20, the trust bank dealer says. The USD/CHF also rises to a six-week high of 0.9407 from an earlier low of 0.9352. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0559 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The South Korea won gives up early gains and is likely to end Thursday with losses, as traders buy the dollar on dips, betting that tonight's U.S. ADP jobs report will indicate strong growth in private employment. USD/KRW rebounded to 1,100 from a trough of 1,089.3, and could test the top of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel at 1,105 next. An upside surprise to the U.S. ADP data may cue a strong U.S. non-farm payrolls number Friday that could sway the Federal Reserve toward reducing monetary stimulus this month. USD/KRW is now 1,098.0.(ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] UOBKayHian expects investors to remain cautious on Malaysia until key data points and events cement a trend that the current account will remain in surplus. The 2Q current account surplus was at 1.1% of GDP, shrinking by a third from a year earlier. "We do not foresee the [benchmark] FBMKLCI entering a bearish phase," the house says in a note, while tipping the index at 1760 as of year-end, based on a 14.6X long-term prospective PE. The house keeps Overweight on telecommunications, gaming, construction and oil and gas sectors with Digi.com (6947.KU) and SapuraKencana Petroleum (5218.KU) among top picks. It is Underweight on autos and banking stocks. KLCI is +0.3% at 1722.76 at the midday break. (abhrajit.gangopadhyay@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  is suspected to have sold $100 million so far on Thursday to slow the USD/IDR rise as investors remain doubtful of the prospects of an improvement in Indonesia's current-account deficit. The pair is now at IDR11,600--where \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 --vs IDR11,125 late on Wednesday. "I expect \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to sell dollars just before the Jakarta session ends trying to bring the pair down close to IDR11,125," a dealer says. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee surges for a second day, rallying as much as 2.4% to 65.53 from Wednesday's closing of 67.07 as the market reacts to fresh policy moves by new Reserve Bank of India governor \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  to boost USD liquidity in the onshore market. Thursday's rally follows Wednesday's 2.3% kneejerk reaction to the RBI governor's announcement coupled with the RBI's aggressive dollar-selling in the spot market to boost the INR. USD/INR, now at 66.35, could head to the base of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel at 64.30 if it ends the day below psychological support at 66.00.(ewen.chew@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 U.S. jobs data remains the key risk for the NZD/USD over the next couple of days, says \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions Corporate Dealer Chris Hunter. "People are busy squaring up ahead of a busy 36 hours with a raft of employment data coming out of the U.S." He says the market is expecting jobs data to remain positive and thus is expected to provide support for the U.S. dollar. "A disappointment would work in the Kiwi's favor, but it would have to be fairly disappointing because it's going to take more than one disappointing release out of the U.S. for the market to change its view" that tapering is imminent. The pair is at 0.7890 vs 0.7910 early. Hunter puts short-term support at 0.7875, with resistance coming in at 0.7930. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 PREVIEW: China's exports likely grew 6% in August compared with the same month last year, according to the median forecast of 11 economists surveyed by \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . That would be a small pick-up from July's 5.1% growth and shows that China can expect to reap some modest benefits from the ongoing recovery in the developed world. Still, exports are likely to play a much smaller role in driving the economy than they have in the past. "The underlying export momentum has likely picked up further, as predicted by the improving U.S. and European economies," says Wang Tao at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "However, the strength of China's exports may continue to be constrained by renminbi appreciation." Imports likely rose 11.7% on year in August, according to the same survey, compared with 10.9% in July. China's monthly trade surplus may have edged up to $20.4 billion from $17.8 billion, the poll predicts. (richard.silk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 The recent surge in oil prices due to the ongoing Syrian crisis comes at a particularly bad time for several Asian countries facing high inflation, large fuel subsidies and widening oil-trade deficits, analyst Hak Bin Chua at BofA \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  said in a note. The most vulnerable are Thailand, Korea and India who spend the largest percentage of their GDP on oil imports, while Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand provide the heaviest fuel subsidies in the region. "Rising global oil prices would also increase fuel subsidy costs and strain the fiscal deficits for several ASEAN countries," Mr. Chua said. He said Indonesia's oil-trade deficit has ballooned to $2.8 billion in July, and Malaysia's oil-trade surplus flipped into a small deficit earlier this year. "A worst-case scenario could trigger another round of fuel price hikes, fanning inflation pressures," he added. (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0335 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  decision to stand pat on its monetary policy and an upgrade in its economic assessment are in line with market expectations, JPMorgan Securities Japan chief rates strategist Takafumi Yamawaki says. The economy "is recovering moderately," the policy board said. "As economic indicators have shown Japan's economy is improving. The BOJ's upgrade in its assessment was expected," Yamawaki adds. The JGB market is already factoring in a consumption-tax hike and the BOJ announcement will unlikely have a large impact on the government's decision on the tax increase, he says. "Afternoon trade will unlikely move on the BOJ's announcement," Yamawaki says. The benchmark 10-year JGB yield is unchanged at 0.770%. Lead September JGB futures are up 0.05 at 144.02. (yumi.otagaki@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 The USD/CNY falls after the PBOC set the central parity lower at 6.1696 vs Wednesday's 6.1729 to reflect the dollar's broad weakness. The pair is at 6.1185 vs 6.1201 late Wednesday. Traders say they would keep an eye for economic policy pledges from G20 meeting in St Petersburg for any trading cues, but added that their focus remains on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily fixing levels and China's domestic economy. "The daily fixing levels have been stable in recent weeks and this translates to a narrow trading band. We don't see the same downward pressure on the yuan compared to other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the rupee," says a Shanghai-based trader at a local bank. He tips the pair to trade in a 6.1170-6.1230 range for the rest of the session. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.2280/6.2310 from 6.2340/6.2380 late Wednesday. (esther.fung@wsj.com) [ 05-09-13 0653GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130905e99500089\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
