\par\par \b \uc2 India, Indonesia  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Continue to Slide\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sudeep Jain and Ben Otto   \par \uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2013\par 14:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Indian and Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were battered Wednesday, with both the rupee and the rupiah continuing a recent downtrend, reflecting investors' concerns over growing trade imbalances and slowing emerging-market economies.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slumped to within a whisker of a record low before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 --according to traders--aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to its lowest level in more than four years.\par \par \uc2 The two countries have taken the brunt of a selloff in Asia, as their widening trade deficits and rising inflation leave them especially vulnerable to the impact of global fund managers withdrawing investments from Asia amid signs of accelerating growth in the U.S. and Europe. The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  and \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  have pursued a variety of strategies to reverse the trend, including raising interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but so far they have failed to stem the slide.\par \par \uc2 India's rupee was at 67.10 to the U.S. dollar on Wednesday afternoon after reaching 68.60 earlier in the day--close to a record 68.80 hit on Aug. 28.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold dollars several times in recent weeks. The RBI says it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market only to curb volatility and not to target a particular exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's slide--it is down 19% in the year to date--comes amid a change in leadership at the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 , with former International Monetary Fund Chief Economist \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  set to commence his duties as governor on Thursday. His first order of business will likely be to stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's rupiah reached 11,125 against the dollar in the interbank market on Wednesday, its lowest level since April 2009. It is down 14% in the year to date, and the outlook is for the downtrend to continue, with one-month non-deliverable forwards pricing in a further 6% decline.\par \par \uc2 For people looking to buy dollars from money changers and banks, the rupiah is looking even weaker. The difference between the levels that banks use with each other and posted rates at exchange counters have widened to as high as an extra 900 rupiah per dollar compared with about 200 under normal conditions. Analysts say this is a sign of fear that the supply of U.S. notes is low and indicative of a large appetite to exchange rupiah for dollars.\par \par \uc2 Signs on Wednesday that the U.S. is moving closer to military action against Syria could make the situation worse for both India and Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Although crude-oil prices have slipped since President Barack Obama said over the weekend that he would seek Congress's approval for a strike, support from key political players increases the likelihood of congressional assent, which could spur prices higher. That is bad news for both India and Indonesia, as it threatens to boost their already-high energy-import bills.\par \par \uc2 Outflows from Asia, especially Indonesia and India, picked up last week to the most since June, when markets were roiled by the first signs that the Federal Reserve was poised to end aggressive bond buying sooner than previously anticipated. Global investors withdrew $104 million from Indonesia's bond markets in the week ending Aug. 28, the most of any Asian debt market, according to research from \uc2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group Ltd\uc2 . In India, fund managers have been exiting stock markets in greater numbers, with $213 million leaving the nation's equities over the same period.\par \par \uc2 --Ashutosh Joshi and Andreas Ismar contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sudeep Jain at sudeep.jain@wsj.com and \uc2 Ben Otto\uc2  at ben.otto@wsj.com [ 04-09-13 1202GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130904e994000f1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
