\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Asia\b0\par\par\uc2 6,395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2013\par 03:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE--The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY    6.1127-6.1200   6.1090-6.1300 \par USD/TWD    29.950-30.000   29.900-30.060 \par USD/KRW     1,108-1,112     1,100-1,116 \par USD/SGD    1.2721-1.2790   1.2700-1.2800 \par USD/MYR    3.2800-3.3000   3.2570-3.3070 \par USD/THB     32.00-32.20     31.80-32.28 \par USD/PHP     44.50-44.80     44.38-45.00 \par USD/IDR    10,900-11,000   10,800-11,500 \par USD/INR     65.48-66.00     65.00-66.50 \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/CNY - consolidation. The PBOC's USD/CNY parity rate could be set higher on account of the stronger greenback, but the spot USD/CNY may not follow higher. The yuan is likely to find support from the official China August PMI, released Sunday, which continues to affirm an improving economy that is transitioning toward a more sustainable growth pattern. The \uc2 HSBC\uc2  August China PMI will be released today, which could confirm that improving trend in the official index. As the China economy stabilizes, investors will expect further financial liberalization this year, which will add to demand for the yuan. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for the USD/CNY is at 6.1127 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1090 (record low). Immediate resistance is likely at 6.1200 (psychological resistance), then at 6.1212 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1300 (20-day Bollinger mid and psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/TWD - consolidation, downside bias. The USD/TWD continues to look rangebound on the charts, and likely to remain within the consolidation range of 29.900-30.100 despite the broadly stronger USD. Taiwan August manufacturing PMI to be released slightly later will reveal if the Taiwan economy is gaining ground along with the recent improvement in China's manufacturing sector. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 29.950 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 29.900 (psychological support), before 29.830 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 29.940 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 30.000 (psychological resistance), before 30.060 (20-day Bollinger mid).\par \par \uc2 USD/KRW - consolidation with downside bias. The USD/KRW slips to the bottom of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, which ought to support the pair for now, as the \uc2 HSBC\uc2  August PMI comes in at 47.5 versus 47.2 in July. The improved reading is helping to mitigate the disappointing industrial output data released last week, and thus helping the won to strengthen again in spite of a broadly stronger USD. As long as the USD/KRW stays under 1,112, the Bollinger downtrend channel will continue to guide it lower. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,108 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,100 (psychological support), before 1,095 (base of weekly Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1,112 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,116 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance), before 1,120 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/SGD - consolidation, possible bounce. The USD/SGD has found an interim base at the 20-day Bollinger mid at 1.2720, and could bounce higher toward the Bollinger uptrend channel at 1.2790 as the greenback gains on Syria tensions and the looming decision by the Federal Reserve to withdraw QE. The Sing dollar could suffer slightly with risk sentiment likely to be shaky as the U.S. contemplates a Syria airstrike, with or without its allies. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2721 (20-day Bollinger mid support), before 1.2700 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2790 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2800 (psychological resistance), before 1.2865 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/MYR - possible bounce. The USD/MYR has hit the 20-day Bollinger mid support at 3.2800 and could turn firmer from here due to buying on dips with the increased likelihood of the U.S. Federal Reserve tapering quantitative easing this month. The dollar is slightly firmer after U.S. data on Friday reaffirmed that the U.S. economy is indeed on a solid footing, which could thus convince the Federal Reserve to start withdrawing QE this month. The USD/MYR could bounce off the 20-day Bollinger mid and head back toward the Bollinger uptrend channel at 3.3070. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2800 (psychological support and 20-day Bollinger mid), then at 3.2570 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 3.2500 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 3.3000 (psychological barrier), then at 3.3070 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 3.3350 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/THB - consolidation. The USD/THB is likely to drift about in a 32.00-32.20 range as investors sit on the sidelines watching Syria and awaiting the Federal Reserve decision this month on the possible withdrawal of QE. Without any catalyst, and without any strong chart signals, the baht may consolidate within a narrow range for now. One domestic front to watch could be the dissent arising from Thai rubber farmers who are demanding the government buys from them at artificially high prices, as it does from the rice growers. A mass protest is planned for Sep. 3. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.00 (psychological support), then at 31.80 (psychological support), before 31.69 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 32.20 (psychological resistance), then at 32.28 (Wednesday's 3-year high), before 32.49 (monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\par \par \uc2 USD/PHP - possible pullback lower. The USD/PHP may pullback slightly as risk sentiment improves on uplifting regional economic data. China PMI released Sunday showed an improvement over its previous month, as did South Korea's \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI released earlier Monday. The USD/PHP could slip back down to the upper Bollinger band (standard deviation 2) at 44.38 and then consolidate. The peso has been wilting recently due to broad dollar strength, despite healthy 2Q Philippines GDP data released last week meeting expectations at 7.5%. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.50 (psychological support), then at 44.38 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2), before 44.20 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.80 (psychological resistance), then at 45.00 (psychological resistance), before 45.21 (monthly Ichimoku Cloud resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/IDR - consolidation. The USD/IDR appears to be stabilizing, after the Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in a special meeting last week, hiked the benchmark interest rate by 50 basis points to 7.0%. The move was seen as an attempt to pull the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from its recent plunge, and also to address rising inflation pressures. But analysts think that immediate concern that plagues the rupiah in the open market is the lack of transparency and price discovery, which is why the NDF market still prices the USD/IDR at an exorbitant premium - a sign of poor investor confidence. The premium that the 1-month USD/IDR NDF contract commands over the spot price came down sharply - in reaction to \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's move - to IDR500 from almost IDR800 Wednesday, but it still remains very elevated considering it trades at around IDR20-IDR40 in stable market conditions. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for the spot USD/IDR is at 10,900 (psychological support), then at 10,800 (psychological support), before 10,500 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 11,000 (major psychological barrier), then at 11,500 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 USD/INR - consolidation lower. The USD/INR could drift lower as the rupee finally reacts to the government's piecemeal attempts to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The latest move, which traders deem could be the most effective immediate measure to boost the rupee, has removed the main source of USD demand from the open market - the state-run oil companies. The RBI will now sell dollars directly to the 3 state-run oil firms, and thus eliminate their buying in the spot market which drives the USD/INR higher and thus depresses the INR. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 65.48 (upper Bollinger band standard deviation 2), then at 65.00 (psychological support), before 64.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 66.00 (psychological resistance), then at 66.50 (psychological resistance), before 67.00 (psychological resistance).\par \par \uc2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.) [ 02-09-13 0141GMT ]\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE--The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs in Asia today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY    6.1125-6.1200   6.1090-6.1293 \par USD/TWD    29.820-29.940   29.650-29.990 \par USD/KRW    1,092-1,100     1,090-1,106 \par USD/SGD    1.2720-1.2795   1.2690-1.2800 \par USD/MYR    3.2590-2.2830   3.2500-3.3000 \par USD/THB    32.00-32.20     31.80-32.28 \par USD/PHP    44.42-44.50     44.20-44.80 \par USD/IDR    10,900-11,000   10,800-11,500 \par USD/INR    66.00-66.50     65.80-67.00 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CNY - consolidation with downside bias. USD/CNY is likely to trend lower toward 6.1125, the Bollinger downtrend channel suggests. But there is a risk of an upside surprise in the PBOC's USD/CNY parity rate, due to the steep rise in USD/JPY and the ICE dollar index overnight. The USD/CNY NDF market is apparently betting on a lower USD/CNY fixing when the G-20 meeting begins Thursday, as China typically sets the yuan stronger before international economic conferences. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for USD/CNY is at 6.1125 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1090 (record low). Immediate resistance is likely at 6.1200 (psychological resistance), then at 6.1209 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1293 (20-day Bollinger mid).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/TWD - downside bias. USD/TWD breaks lower and enters the daily Bollinger downtrend channel at 29.940, which will now cap the pair. The dollar, though slightly stronger on the whole, may continue to slip against the TWD - due to proxy gains from the yuan, which has managed to stay strong despite emerging market rout. USD/TWD could next head toward the 20-week Bollinger mid at 29.650. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 29.820 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 29.650 (20-week Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is likely at 29.940 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 29.990 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance), before 30.000 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/KRW - downside bias. USD/KRW breaks the psychological barrier at 1,100 and slumps to 1,096.4 on stoploss orders triggered. The won has been gaining over the past few sessions due to inflows from foreign equity investors and large ship-building orders. The move has gained momentum this week and may continue lower despite USD/JPY heading higher; the yen and the won typically move in tandem. The next stop for USD/KRW could be the base of the weekly Bollinger downtrend channel at 1,092. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,092 (base of weekly Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,090 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1,100 (psychological barrier), then at 1,106 (top of weekly Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,110 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/SGD - consolidation. USD/SGD may have bottomed for now at the 20-day Bollinger mid at 1.2720, and could gain additional support from the USD/JPY spike overnight. If the Bollinger mid support holds up again Tuesday, chances will increase that the greenback could bounce higher toward the Bollinger uptrend channel at 1.2795. But if the Bollinger mid support breaks on a daily closing basis, the Ichimoku Cloud support at 1.2690 beckons. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2720 (20-day Bollinger mid support), then at 1.2700 (psychological support), before 1.2690 (daily Ichimoku Cloud support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2795 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), then at 1.2800 (psychological resistance), before 1.2867 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/MYR - consolidation lower. USD/MYR has breached the 20-day Bollinger mid support at 3.2800 could be on its way down to 3.2590 where it will next find a firmer base. The Malaysia government Monday announced a cut to the fuel subsidy effective immediately, which has long been in place to buffer citizens from rising energy inflation, but has been burning a hole in the government's pocket. The move is seen as a response to criticisms of the budget deficit that the country has been running for 15 years straight. Cutting the subsidy will shore up the government's fiscal situation, which ought to instill more confidence among the foreign debt holders - who hold close to half of all government bonds. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2590 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 3.2500 (psychological support), before 3.2340 (bottom of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 3.2830 (20-day Bollinger mid resistance), then at 3.3000 (psychological barrier), before 3.3070 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/THB - consolidation lower. USD/THB could break the 32.00 psychological barrier as regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rise against the greenback, spurred by sharp gains in the South Korea won, which is a regional risk indicator. On a break of the 32.00 support, the pair may range within 31.71-32.00. However, a factor that may crimp baht gains is the dissent arising from Thai rubber farmers who are demanding the government buys from them at artificially high prices, as it does from the rice growers. A mass protest is planned Tuesday. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.00 (psychological support), then at 31.80 (psychological support), before 31.71 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 32.20 (psychological resistance), then at 32.28 (Wednesday's 3-year high), before 32.49 (monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/PHP - pullback lower. USD/PHP could drop back inside the confines of the daily Bollinger uptrend channel which currently spans 44.01-44.42. The pair has recently been trading outside the upper Bollinger band (standard deviation 2), which indicates an overbought skew to the dollar, and this could be time for a pullback, as regional USD/Asia pairs all retreat. The peso ought to hold up better due to its encouraging 2Q GDP growth of 7.5%, but it has also suffered in recent weeks as investors attempt to factor in the impending Federal Reserve pullout from quantitative easing. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.42 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2), then at 44.20 (psychological support), before 44.01 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.50 (psychological resistance), then at 44.80 (psychological resistance), before 45.00 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/IDR - consolidation lower. USD/IDR could drift a tad lower as neighboring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which have been under pressure lately, such as the Malaysia ringgit, recover sharply. The pair could test the 10,900 psychological support next. The rupiah appears to be stabilizing, after the Indonesia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a special meeting last week, hiked the benchmark interest rate by 50 basis points to 7.0%. The move was seen as an attempt to pull the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from its recent plunge, and also to address rising inflation pressures. However, the NDF market still prices the USD/IDR at an exorbitant premium - a sign of poor investor confidence. The premium that the 1-month USD/IDR NDF contract is still at IDR500 versus its typical range of IDR20-IDR40 in stable market conditions. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 10,900 (psychological support), then at 10,800 (psychological support), before 10,500 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 11,000 (major psychological barrier), then at 11,500 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/INR - consolidation lower. USD/INR could drift lower as investor confidence improves again a second day. Though India's August PMI dropped to 48.5 from 50.1 in July, indicating the manufacturing sector shrank for the first time in 4.5 years, the data was not unexpected given the slew of poor numbers prior and the recent exodus of foreign investors. Hence the rupee's reaction was fairly mild. The government's latest move to support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  removes the need for the three large state-run oil companies to buy dollar from the spot market, which thus eliminates a huge source of dollar demand that sometimes acts against the rupee. When the oil importers buy USD/INR in large amounts on the spot market, it drives the dollar higher and thus the rupee weaker. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 66.00 (psychological support), then at 65.80 (upper Bollinger band standard deviation 2), before 65.00 (psychological support) Immediate resistance is at 66.50 (psychological resistance), before 67.00 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.) [ 03-09-13 0045GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE--The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs in Asia today:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par          Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY   6.1206-6.1288   6.1125-6.1300 \par USD/TWD   29.820-29.930   29.650-29.990 \par USD/KRW   1,100-1,106     1,098-1,108 \par USD/SGD   1.2730-1.2800   1.2700-1.2875 \par USD/MYR   3.2860-2.3000   3.2800-3.3100 \par USD/THB   32.00-32.20     31.80-32.28 \par USD/PHP   44.47-44.50     44.20-44.75 \par USD/IDR   11,000-11,500   10,900-12,000 \par USD/INR   67.50-68.00     67.00-68.80 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CNY - consolidation, mild upside bias. Another leg higher in USD/JPY makes it more likely that the PBOC will move the USD/CNY parity rate higher, possibly towards 6.1750. The overall rise of the greenback overnight, following better-than-expected U.S. manufacturing data released overnight, also may add to some dollar bullishness in spot USD/CNY. But the start of the G20 summit this Thursday will keep expectations alive for a lower USD/CNY fixing in days to come. China typically sets the yuan stronger before international economic conferences, so as not to rile U.S. accusations of undervaluing its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for USD/CNY is at 6.1206 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.1125 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1090 (record low). Immediate resistance is likely at 6.1288 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 6.1300 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/TWD - consolidation with downside bias. USD/TWD is likely to linger near the base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel at 29.820, as the dollar firms up a tad overnight due to the rise of the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield. The Taiwan dollar is likely to rally over the course of the week, as long as the USD/TWD remains within the Bollinger downtrend channel which now caps at 29.930. The proxy effect of the yuan, which has been an anchor through the emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  selloff, ought to help keep the Taiwan dollar moored. USD/TWD could next head towards the 20-week Bollinger mid at 29.650. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 29.820 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 29.650 (20-week Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is likely at 29.930 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 29.990 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance), before 30.000 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/KRW - relief rally. USD/KRW gaps up Wednesday, opening above the psychological barrier of 1,100 as short-covering begins, prompted by stronger U.S. manufacturing data which strengthens the case for the Federal Reserve to reduce monetary stimulus this month. The dollar is broadly higher as the U.S. Treasuries got sold off overnight, sending the benchmark 10-year yield up in the process. USD/KRW may target the top of the Bollinger downtrend channel at 1,108, but as long as it ends the day below this mark, the downside bias should remain intact. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,100 (psychological support), then at 1,098 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,092 (base of weekly Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 1,106 (top of weekly Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,108 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,110 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/SGD - consolidation. USD/SGD spiked higher overnight on the stronger U.S. manufacturing data, which adds to the likelihood that the Federal Reserve will taper quantitative easing this month. The dollar is likely to range between 1.2731-1.2800 for now, supported by the 20-day Bollinger mid and capped by the psychological barrier above - which is also the entrance to the Bollinger uptrend channel. Data released Tuesday showed Singapore's August manufacturing PMI fell to 50.5 from 51.8 in July - a number that still reflects expansion of the sector. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2730 (20-day Bollinger mid support), then at 1.2700 (psychological support), before 1.2693 (daily Ichimoku Cloud support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2800 (psychological resistance and daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1.2875 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/MYR - possible upside bias. USD/MYR bounced back above 20-day Bollinger mid at 3.2860, creating a mild bullish technical signal. This signal could increase in strength if the pair ends Wednesday above 3.3100 and therefore inside the Bollinger uptrend channel. The greenback got a boost from the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield rising overnight as bonds got sold off on the better-than-expected U.S manufacturing data. The ringgit may however find support from punters betting on further action from the Malaysia government to improve the country's fiscal situation, in a follow-up to the cutting of fuel subsidies announced earlier. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2860 (20-day Bollinger mid), then at 3.2800 (psychological support), before 3.2630 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 3.3000 (psychological barrier), then at 3.3100 (daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 3.3330 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/THB - consolidation. USD/THB could range between 32.00-32.20, buoyed by U.S. dollar strength arising from higher \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields. The dollar rose overnight on better-than-expected U.S. manufacturing data, which triggered a bond selloff in expectation of likely Fed tapering at the FOMC meeting this month. The Thai government failed to quell a domestic unrest despite offering to cut rubber export levies, and having the exporters pass the savings on to the farmers. The rubber farmers have been protesting for a subsidy program akin to that for the rice farmers. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.00 (psychological support), then at 31.80 (psychological support), before 31.75 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 32.20 (psychological resistance), then at 32.28 (Wednesday's 3-year high), before 32.49 (monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/PHP - consolidation higher. USD/PHP may head back up again toward its 3-year high of 44.75 as the greenback recovers sharply overnight. The respite in dollar strength was short-lived after upbeat U.S manufacturing data Tuesday, which makes it seem more likely the Fed will taper monetary stimulus this month. Foreign investors, who were betting heavily on the peso because of the credit rating upgrades to "investment" level earlier this year, may now be rushing for the exit, fearing a mass exodus once the end of U.S. QE truly begins. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.47 (daily Bollinger band standard deviation 2), then at 44.20 (psychological support), before 44.05 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.50 (psychological resistance), then at 44.75 (3-year high), before 44.80 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/IDR - consolidation lower. USD/IDR is on a path toward 11,500, after having risen above the psychological trading barrier of 11,000 again to mark a new 4-year high - a reaction to much worse than expected Indonesia trade deficit for July. The trade deficit jumped to a record $2.3 billion versus consensus for a shortfall of $0.3 billion. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to cap gains intermittently on the way up, but there is little reason to expect the rupiah to recover for now, unless the government implements further policy action in a follow-up to the impromptu rate hike last week. Indonesia exports falling by 6.1%, as imports rose by 6.5% attributed to the massive trade imbalance. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 11,000 (psychological support), then at 10,900 (psychological support), before 10,800 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 11,500 (psychological resistance), before 12,000 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/INR - upside bias. USD/INR looks ready to pop higher to possibly a new record high, helped by better-than-expected U.S. manufacturing data released overnight. The strong bullish candlestick Tuesday is a technical indicator that the market is still fearful of further rupee weakness. Jittery investors pushed the rupee past the 68.00 barrier again, as USD/INR spiked to 68.25 before subsiding, which reveals that the topside may still be the weaker side for the USD/INR. The current record low for the rupee is 68.80, notched last Wednesday. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 67.50 (psychological support), then at 67.00 (psychological support), before 66.27 (upper Bollinger band standard deviation 2). Immediate resistance is at 68.00(psychological resistance), then at 68.50 (psychological resistance), before 68.80 (record high).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.) [ 04-09-13 0047GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE--The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine secondary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs in Asia today:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par           Immediate Range  Larger Range \par USD/CNY   6.1204-6.1283   6.1126-6.1300 \par USD/TWD   29.820-29.930   29.650-29.990 \par USD/KRW   1,091-1,100     1,090-1,105 \par USD/SGD   1.2738-1.2800   1.2700-1.2866 \par USD/MYR   3.2640-2.2800   3.2500-3.2860 \par USD/THB   32.00-32.20     31.80-32.28 \par USD/PHP   44.20-44.50     44.09-44.51 \par USD/IDR   11,000-11,500   10,900-12,000 \par USD/INR   66.50-67.00     66.00-67.50 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Ranges are calculated using recent highs and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis--Bollinger Bands, Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/CNY - consolidation. The PBOC could set the USD/CNY parity rate lower Thursday as the G-20 summit begins - a form of window dressing to make the yuan appear stronger before key meetings with the U.S. Another reason for a possibly lower PBOC fixing is that the dollar fell overnight against the majors, though it rose slightly against USD/JPY. As such, USD/CNY could consolidate around its current level, barring an unexpectedly low USD/CNY parity rate. Fundamentally, the yuan is likely to remain strong within the region, due to signs of pickup in the economy even as restructuring is ongoing. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for USD/CNY is at 6.1204 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 6.1126 (base of Bollinger downtrend channel), before 6.1090 (record low). Immediate resistance is likely at 6.1283 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 6.1300 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/TWD - consolidation with downside bias. USD/TWD continues to be drawn lower by the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, which now caps at 29.930, and will likely drift down toward 29.650 Thursday as the greenback subsides in overnight trade. But further downside may be limited due to the rise of the U.S. 10-year Treasury yield back above 2.9%, which suggests the market is expecting the U.S. Federal Reserve to taper its pace of bond purchases later this month. The proxy effect of the yuan, which has been an anchor through the emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  selloff, ought to help keep the Taiwan dollar moored. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 29.820 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), before 29.650 (20-week Bollinger mid). Immediate resistance is likely at 29.930 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 29.990 (daily Ichimoku Cloud resistance), before 30.000 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/KRW - downside bias. The South Korea won continues to be the outperformer in the region, and as technical momentum builds for a stronger won, USD/KRW is likely to keep heading lower. However, the pair may have reached some mid-term support levels, which could stall for now. The dollar is close to the base of the weekly Bollinger downtrend channel, and also the entrance to the monthly Bollinger downtrend channel. The coincidence of these two support levels at around 1,190-1,191 could impede gains for the KRW. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  Thursday highlighted the country's robust export growth despite external headwinds, which could add to bullish sentiment for the won. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 1,091 (base of weekly Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 1,090 (monthly Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,080 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 1,100 (psychological resistance), then at 1,105 (top of weekly Bollinger downtrend channel), before 1,106 (top of daily Bollinger downtrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/SGD - consolidation. As the greenback slipped overnight, USD/SGD finds itself still trapped within the 1.2738-1.2800 span for now, bound by the 20-day Bollinger mid support and the psychological barrier above - which is also the entrance to the Bollinger uptrend channel. The next cue for a larger move in USD/SGD could come from USD/CNY - where punters expect a much stronger yuan valuation ahead of the G-20 summit that begins today. But on the flipside, if USD/JPY breaks above 100.00, USD/SGD may follow higher. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate support is at 1.2738 (20-day Bollinger mid support), then at 1.2700 (psychological support), before 1.2693 (daily Ichimoku Cloud support). Immediate resistance is at 1.2800 (psychological resistance and daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 1.2866 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/MYR - consolidation lower. USD/MYR could drift slightly lower and then consolidate within a 3.2640-3.2860 range, after the pair closed Wednesday below the 20-day Bollinger mid - a mild bearish signal. Ratings agencies Fitch and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  have both noted that the Malaysia government's attempt at fiscal correction via cutting of the fuel subsidy may not be enough; though \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it was a step in the right direction. The government's decisive response to the recent spotlight on its dwindling current account deficit may be reason to expect the ringgit to recover further. Punters are betting on further action from the Malaysia government to improve the country's fiscal situation, with the possibility of a consumption tax to be introduced at the October budget announcement. Traders expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely keep the benchmark interest rate unchanged at its policy meeting today. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 3.2640 (daily Bollinger downtrend channel), then at 3.2500 (psychological support), before 3.2420 (base of daily Bollinger downtrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 3.2800 (psychological resistance), then at 3.2860 (20-day Bollinger mid), before 3.3000 (psychological barrier).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/THB - consolidation. USD/THB may continue to hover between 32.00-32.20, balanced by U.S. dollar strength arising from higher \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields, and improving investor sentiment in Asia. The U.S. 10-year Treasury yield rose back above 2.90% as bonds got sold again due to increasing likelihood of the Fed tapering quantitative easing this month. But Asia is looking up as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which have been under immense selling pressure lately pull back from the brink of multi-year lows, as a string of improving manufacturing PMIs boost confidence. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 32.00 (psychological support), then at 31.80 (psychological support), before 31.79 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel). Immediate resistance is at 32.20 (psychological resistance), then at 32.28 (last Wednesday's 3-year high), before 32.49 (monthly Bollinger uptrend channel).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/PHP - consolidation lower. USD/PHP slips back inside the confines of the Bollinger uptrend channel, after having spent the most part of last week in overbought territory. The peso is likely to recover further Thursday, helped by improving investor sentiment in the region. But a dollar dip could stall at the base of the Bollinger uptrend channel at 44.09. As long as the pair remains above this level, an upside bias remains in the near term. As the G-20 summit begins, markets will be awaiting news on the U.S.-Syria situation developing for hints of risk aversion once more. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 44.20 (psychological support), then at 44.09 (base of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 44.00 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is likely at 44.50 (psychological resistance), then at 44.51 (top of daily Bollinger uptrend channel), before 44.75 (3-year high).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/IDR - consolidation lower. Spot USD/IDR still appears buoyant as long as it stays above the 11,000 psychological barrier, but the USD/IDR NDF market may selloff slightly due to improving market sentiment. The rally in U.S. stocks and improving global manufacturing PMIs this week have been helping to offset fears of military escalation in Syria, and the Fed tapering of quantitative easing. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support for spot USD/IDR is at 11,000 (psychological support), then at 10,900 (psychological support), before 10,800 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 11,500 (psychological resistance), before 12,000 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 USD/INR - upside bias. The India rupee jumped Wednesday on fresh measures announced by the new Reserve Bank of India governor. USD/INR could be on its way to 66.00 and possibly lower as investors react to the announcements. Raghuram Rajan, in his first speech as governor, announced that banks which receive dollars into foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deposits from Indians living abroad now will be able to swap those dollars for rupees from the RBI, and banks can now raise double the previous amount that was allowed via the issuing of overseas bonds. The dollars received can then be exchanged for rupees at cheaper-than-market rates with the RBI. The new policies, in place till Nov. 30, increase dollar liquidity in the system, thus USD/INR fell sharply in anticipation of more USD supply to hit the market soon. Dow Jones technical analysis suggests immediate support is at 66.50 (psychological support), then at 66.00 (psychological support), before 65.50 (psychological support). Immediate resistance is at 67.00(psychological resistance), then at 67.50 (psychological resistance), before 68.00 (psychological resistance).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ewen Chew at ewen.chew@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.) [ 05-09-13 0050GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1136   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130902e9920001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
