\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CHAT: USD/IDR Higher, But Suspected  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Slows Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,943 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2013\par 11:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0909 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/IDR remains well bid but suspected \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still capping the pair below the psychologically important level of 11,000. The pair now is at 10,900 vs 10,850 late Monday in the interbank market, but banks mostly sell the U.S. dollar to their customers already above 11,200, dealers say. Three dealers suspected the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of having sold around $100 million to defend the local unit. "Dollar offer is very scanty, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 's capacity to sell dollars is dwindling," one of them says. Many economists have suggested \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  further raise interest rates to help slow capital outflows. Elsewhere, the government sold IDR12 trillion of bonds in auction, exceeding its IDR8 trillion target as it received IDR23 trillion in total bids. Meanwhile, local stocks decline nearly 3.6%. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] USD/TWD is higher in thin trade, mostly lifted by foreign investor buying, say two local traders. The pair is at 30.060 versus 30.015 late Monday in Taipei. The traders suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of buying around 29.980 to keep the pair in its target band. "Although foreign fund outflows will likely continue on Wednesday, exporters' selling near the month-end will still cap the greenback's rise," a trader says. The second trader says some foreign investor buying is related to their remittance of stock dividends. They tip the pair to trade in a 29.960 to 30.000 band Wednesday. Taiwan shares end 0.9% lower, with foreign investors net sellers of NT$695 million worth of local shares. Taiwan government bonds are steady in quiet trade ahead of Thursday's new 10-year bond starting its when-issued trade. (fanny.liu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0807 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The USD/KRW rises to 1,116.3 vs 1,112.7 late Monday in Seoul, tracking the greenback's broad gains versus regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as financial markets fret about possible U.S. military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Syria. Export settlements near the month-end have been easily digested by purchases by offshore players who bet on the USD's near-term uptrend, traders say. Analysts tip the USD/KRW in a 1,110-1,120 range for Wednesday. (in-soo.nam@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Sudeep Jain And Ashutosh Joshi \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI--The Indian rupee fell to a fresh record low against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday, as growing expectations of a U.S. military \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Syria pushed investors to seek safety in the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee fell to as low as 65.93 to the U.S. dollar, down sharply from 64.30 late Monday in Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee's decline led to a sharp selloff in stocks, reversing the gains of the last three sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bombay Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 's S&P BSE Sensex fell 2.4%, to 18110.70 points by Tuesday afternoon. The index has lost more than 5% this month, making it the worst-performing major stock market in Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Equity markets only look at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and nothing else," said Shardul Kulkarni, analyst at Mumbai brokerage Angel Broking Ltd.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has fallen more than 17% against the dollar since the start of May over fears that India will struggle to fund its wide current account deficit if the U.S. Federal Reserve winds down its easy-money policy. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers say that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  likely sold dollars in the previous two sessions to prevent the rupee's slide against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Tuesday, analysts said the dollar was higher against many emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . In India specifically, "the passage of the food security bill has also hurt sentiment," said Rajeev Darji, an analyst at XDirect Global Solutions, an online \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading firm in Mumbai.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's lower house of Parliament on Monday passed a bill guaranteeing subsidized grain for nearly 70% of India's population. Analysts fear that this would add to the government's spending and could worsen its financial health.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Darji expects the rupee to weaken to 67.50 to the dollar in coming sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Tuesday, the dollar was also in demand by oil refiners who typically buy more dollars toward the end of month to settle import bills.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In stocks, banks and financial companies were the worst performers Tuesday, as the further weakening of rupee means the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  will likely continue with liquidity tightening measures that have significantly increased the lenders' short-term borrowing costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mortgage lender \f28 \uc2 \f2 Housing Development Finance Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  was down 7.1% to 691.50 rupees ($10.5), while \f28 \uc2 \f2 HDFC Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 5.6% lower at 576.00 rupees.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ---\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Saptarishi Dutta contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Ashutosh Joshi at ashutosh.joshi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0643 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A new record low of 65.71 to the dollar underscores the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian government\f28 \uc2 \f2 's inability to address the rupee's collapse, despite announcing the approval of new government infrastructure projects worth $28.47 billion. The greenback has been rising across the region this week as fear of further risk aversion sets in. The major catalyst for this move has been the potential entry of the U.S. into the Syria conflict. But in the background, an end to U.S. quantitative easing and the associated exit from high-yielding emerging market assets has seen several emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  get dumped in quick succession. The rupee could go on weakening, possibly to the next psychological barrier at 66.00, as investor confidence wanes, not helped by the finance minister's reassurance that the rupee is undervalued and that the government won't breach its fiscal deficit targets. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The USD/JPY hasn't shown any huge reaction to Japanese Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shinzo Abe\f28 \uc2 \f2 's comment on Monday that the government won't exclude a corporate tax cut when considering how to mitigate negative economic impact from a planned two-stage sales tax hike. Hiroshi Maeba, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading Japan for \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Tokyo, says the market is largely unmoved because the government is not seriously thinking about cutting the corporate tax rate. "We also have sales tax hike issues undecided," he adds. Mr. Abe is due to decide in early October whether Japan will go ahead with the sales tax hike. The pair is at 98.19. (takashi.mochizuki@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0528 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A global daily close below 0.8957 would justify a bearish short-term call for the Australia dollar, which has slipped Tuesday to 0.8957 from 0.9038 against a backdrop of global risk aversion. If the Bollinger downtrend channel, which begins at 0.8957, comes into play, the Aussie could fall further to the base of the channel at 0.8870 and then possibly to the psychological support at 0.8800. Fears of a U.S. strike in Syria, and the looming specter of the Federal Reserve winding down its monetary stimulus program, plus the routing of emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has created an air of global risk aversion. Risk-sensitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including the Aussie, have thus suffered this week. The Aussie is currently 0.8965. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The NZD/USD is looking heavy and could test its recent low of 0.7760, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Imre Speizer. Markets are jittery on worries about the Syrian crisis and soft U.S. economic data. Speizer notes that even news that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fonterra Co-Operative Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (FCG.NZ) has again lifted its forecast payout to its farmer shareholders for the current season failed to give the Kiwi a lift. "It's the sentiment on the day, people are selling Kiwi and Aussie," he says. Investors will be watching for more U.S. data later in the global trading day as they try to gauge when the Fed might begin tapering. The pair is at 0.7819 versus 0.7853 early Tuesday in New Zealand. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The NZD/USD is looking heavy and could test its recent low of 0.7760, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Imre Speizer. Markets are jittery on worries about the Syrian crisis and soft U.S. economic data. Speizer notes that even news that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fonterra Co-Operative Group Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (FCG.NZ) has again lifted its forecast payout to its farmer shareholders for the current season failed to give the Kiwi a lift. "It's the sentiment on the day, people are selling Kiwi and Aussie," he says. Investors will be watching for more U.S. data later in the global trading day as they try to gauge when the Fed might begin tapering. The pair is at 0.7819 versus 0.7853 early Tuesday in New Zealand. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The peso declines a third consecutive session, falling to a 2.5-yar low of 44.45 to the U.S. dollar, as the emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  selloff continues around the world. USD/PHP,currently at 44.44, could soon reach the monthly Ichimoku Cloud at 44.47 - which if penetrated, could open the way to the major psychological barrier at 45.00. Traders may be slightly cautious in chasing the move higher though, as the daily chart does look slightly overbought - but this is more likely due to a lag in the technical indicators because of the recent string of Manila market closures. (ewen.chew@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One Singapore dollar now buys as much Malaysia ringgit as it used to in 1998 when the region was in the throes of the Asia Financial Crisis. The Singapore dollar/Malaysia ringgit cross exchange rate, not very often traded for speculation purposes but still watched by Asia traders as a good gauge of regional risk appetite, has hit a 15-year high of 2.5959. The break higher Tuesday illustrates clearly the divergence in Asia markets. On one hand, the countries with current account deficits, or the threat of one appearing in Malaysia's case, are seeing their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stock markets fall to multi-year lows. On the other hand, the more resilient \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of countries with stronger current accounts such as China, South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore are holding up better, despite also losing ground to the U.S. dollar due to the fear of the Fed reducing its bond purchases. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) [ 27-08-13 0910GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0245 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Syrian conflict may spread to neighboring oil producing countries and imperil regional output, rather than affect Syria's own meagre oil production, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  says in a note. "Iraq, currently OPEC's second largest producer, has already seen its security situation significantly deteriorate because of Syria," it said, adding that Iran's support for Assad could also prevent better relations with the US and the return of around 1.5 million bbl/day of Iranian oil to the market. The Syrian conflict is fueling broader sectarian tensions across the entire Middle East, and has the potential to exacerbate tensions in large producing countries with significant Shia populations such as Saudi Arabia and Kuwait, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. It adds that the Syrian conflict comes at a time when unplanned supply outages have already hit a two-year high, and if global oil demand improves later this year, oil prices may move even higher. Nymex crude is up 50c at $106.42/bbl, Brent is up 42c at $111.15/bbl. (eric.yep@dowjones.com) [ 27-08-13 0910GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130827e98r000bo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
