\par\par \b \uc2 DJ 3rd UPDATE: Asian Shares Mixed After Selloff\b0\par\par\uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2013\par 10:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Asian markets mixed after sharp selloff\par \par \uc2 --Shares rebound in Indonesia\par \par \uc2 --Official efforts to buoy markets calm investors\par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Mia Lamar, Prasanta Sahu and Andreas Ismar \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stocks were mixed Wednesday following a dramatic two-day selloff across the region that sent governments in the hardest-hit markets rushing to stem further declines.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's benchmark Jakarta Composite Index was trading nearly 2% higher late afternoon, bouncing back from a sudden rout that sent the index down 9% in two days. Stocks in India were more volatile, rising as much as 1.8% before slipping into the red.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is adding to its woes by appearing to change its policy goals almost from one day to the next," said Nicholas Spiro, managing director of independent research firm Spiro Sovereign Strategy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Officials have accelerated efforts to shore up the country's shaky financial markets, which have for days seen the rupee fall to new lows. The country is likely to go ahead with a plan to issue rupee-denominated bonds overseas, a senior government official told The Wall Street Journal Tuesday, in a bid to attract more capital and stem the rupee's decline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee remained weak Wednesday, trading at 63.73 per U.S. dollar after falling to as low as 64.11 late Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State-driven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was seen in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as well. The country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "continues to remain in the market to stabilize the rupiah exchange rate," Deputy Gov. Perry Warjiyo told the Wall Street Journal, confirming the views of money dealers who had suspected the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was in the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rising expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve will act next month to wind down its $85-billion-a-month bond buying program sparked a global selloff in financial markets this week, hitting Asia's emerging markets the hardest, as they have been among the biggest beneficiaries of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's easy-money policies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is an inflection point for Asia," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Duncan Woolridge. "The last five years have been about getting exposure to Asia's debt-led growth story. That idea is probably finished."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Calmer trade in the U.S. bond market helped sentiment. Yields on the U.S. 10-year note edged back from the 3% mark, although strategists noted countries like Indonesia and India still face a tough road ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Although the Fed's possible liquidity tightening has been blamed, the underlying issue for these countries is a bleak outlook on economic growth for the rest of the year," said Kelly Teoh, market strategist at IG in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo, shares edged up 0.2% to 13,424.33, shaking off earlier losses after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . reported its worst spill of highly radioactive water since the plant suffered a triple meltdown two years ago. Shares of the company, commonly known as Tepco, dropped 9.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sentiment also improved as the dollar regained strength against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , last changing hands at 97.56 yen versus 97.26 yen late Tuesday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks in Hong Kong slumped after a bigger-than-expected rise in consumer prices last month. The Hang Seng Index fell 0.7%, led by declines in interest rate-sensitive property stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade was choppy in Sydney as investors awaited minutes from the U.S. Federal Reserve's July policy meeting later in the trading day. The S&P ASX 200 was last up 0.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares of local mining giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 BHP Billiton\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 2.2% after the company reported its second-straight decline in annual profit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Mia Lamar at mia.lamar@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 21, 2013 04:45 ET (08:45 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130821e98l002ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
