\par\par \b \uc2 4th UPDATE: Asian Shares Sharply Lower On Fed Result, Weak China Data\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2013\par 11:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Asia down on Fed pullback, weak China PMI\par \par \uc2 --Chinese banks lower on liquidity concerns\par \par \uc2 --Dollar hits one year high against peso, ringgit, all-time high against rupee\par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Daniel Inman \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A broad sell-off hit Asian markets Thursday, driving \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stock markets to long-term lows following another bearish reading on China's economy and the strongest indication yet that the U.S. Federal Reserve is poised to begin winding down its bond-buying program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese and China-related stocks tumbled, the Indian rupee hit a record low and gold fell to its lowest price in nearly three years. Stocks in Southeast Asia, where smaller markets are especially vulnerable to outflows of foreign funds, suffered some of the biggest drops. In Indonesia, the Jakarta Composite Index fell 3.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The release of data indicating that China's manufacturing activity contracted again in June accelerated early selling that had begun after the Fed suggested overnight that it would begin cutting back its bond buying by year's end.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Emerging Asia is in a tight corner, between U.S. monetary policy and their own issues," said Angelo Corbetta, head of Asian equities at Pioneer Investments, which had $197.9 billion of assets under management at the end of October. "And most of the silver bullets have been used already."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indications that the Fed could start winding down its stimulus have led to heavy outflows from Asian emerging markets in recent weeks and the selling continued Thursday. The Philippines' PSE Composite was last down 2.9% and Thailand's SET fell 3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese markets fell sharply after a gauge of manufacturing activity fell to its lowest level in nine months. Also weighing on sentiment was a cash crunch among Chinese banks that has pushed interbank lending rates to the highest levels in two years and made banks more cautious about lending.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index lost 2.9%, its worst decline by percentage since July, while the Hang Seng \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Enterprises\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index fell 3.3%. On the mainland, the Shanghai Composite Index lost 2.8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese banks listed in Hong Kong were big losers. \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Construction Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 5.2%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Industrial and Commercial Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  lost 3.8% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Minsheng Banking Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . dropped 6.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Liquidity conditions remain tight, while the latest data shows that China's manufacturing activity continues to contract, putting further pressure on the economy," said Zhang Gang, an analyst at Central China Securities. "Due to a lack of positive catalysts, the market will likely continue to find its bottom."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's preliminary China purchasing managers index for June came in at 48.3, compared to a final reading of 49.2 in May. A figure below 50 indicates a contraction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's benchmark S&P/ASX 200 dived 2.1%, its biggest fall since May 2012. China is Australia's largest trading partner. The Australian dollar plunged to a 33-month low. It has fallen more than 10% this year due to a combination of slowing growth, slumping commodity prices and expectations of interest-rate cuts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo, companies with strong exposure to China underperformed the broader market. Construction and mining equipment manufacturer Komatsu sank 3.8% and industrial robotics firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fanuc Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . dropped 3.2%. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 1.7% despite the U.S. dollar's rise against the yen, which usually benefits Japanese stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar jumped 1.2% against the yen overnight and continued climbing in Asian trading. The dollar was recently at Y98.02, compared with Y96.47 late Wednesday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Selling of equities helped drive down the region's other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which have been under pressure in recent weeks due to outflows prompted by expectations of stronger growth and rising yields in the U.S. The dollar hit one-year highs against the Philippine peso and the Malaysian ringgit. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold fell 3.4% to $1,304.90 a troy ounce, its lowest price since September 2010. Gold saw some of the biggest gains of any asset in recent years because of the Fed's stimulus program, which many thought would trigger high inflation. Gold is considered a hedge against inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Daniel Inman at daniel.inman@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 20-06-13 0957GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130620e96k000g9\par }\